Members of the Welsh Representative Council are afraid they look like fools
Some members of the Representative Council are perplexed by the suggestion that their title should be changed to MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
The controversy began with the plan to change the name of the Council of Representatives to the Welsh Parliament.
Regardless of the party's position, the members of the Representative Council are concerned that the change may make them mockery.
According to one representative of the Workers' Party, the group is concerned that the name “rhymes with the words Twp and Pwp.”
For readers outside of Wales: In the Welsh language, twp means ‘stupid and ‘pwp’ means poop.
According to one spokesman of the Plaid party, the majority of the group ‘is not satisfied’ and has proposed other options.
According to a spokesman of the Conservative Party, his group is “open-minded” about the name change, but points out that there is a short verbal leap from the MWP to a word that refers to a fool.
In Wales, the letter ‘‘‘‘‘‘‘‘‘‘‘‘‘
With regard to the legislative amendment currently being drawn up to the necessary legislative amendment, the Commission stated: ‘Of course, the final decision on titles describing the members of the Representative Council belongs to the members themselves.’
The Welsh Administrative Act of 2017 authorises the Representative Council to change its name.
In June, the Commission published an opinion poll on the proposals, according to which the appointment of the Representative Council as the Welsh Parliament enjoys extensive support.
The Commission favored the members of the Representative Council as Members of the Welsh Parliament or WMP, but the MWP option received the most support in a public opinion poll.
It seems that the members of the Council of Representatives are proposing alternative titles, but the lack of consensus may cause headaches for Chairman Elin Jones, who is expected to introduce a bill on the amendments within a few weeks.
Legislation on the amendments includes other changes in the functioning of the Representative Council, such as rules on the loss of mandate and the format of the committee system.
With regard to legislation, the members of the Council of Representatives may cast their last vote on the title by which they should be invited.
The Macedonians will go to the ballot box in a referendum on changing the country's name
On Sunday, voters will vote on whether the country’s name will be changed to “Northern Macedonian Republic.”
The referendum aims to resolve a decades-long dispute with neighbouring Greece, which also has its own province called Macedonia.
Athens has long reiterated that the northern neighbour's name is, in practice, a regional requirement for the territory that belongs to it, and has repeatedly objected to its EU-NATO membership efforts.
Opposing a referendum, Macedonian President Gjorge Ivanov has said he will ignore the vote.
However, supporters of a referendum such as Prime Minister Zoran Zaevi reason that the name change is simply the price to be paid for EU and NATO membership.
