Shark harms 13-year-old lobster diver in California
Authorities say the shark attacked a 13-year-old boy and harmed him as he dived lobsters on the opening day of the lobster season in California.
The attack took place on Beacon’s Behart in Encinitas just before seven in the morning.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diogo that he dived with his friends on Saturday morning for about half an hour when he heard the boy screaming for help. He and a group of others paddled to the boy to help with the water.
Hammel told that he first thought the boy was just excited after catching a lobster, but then realized he was shouting, ‘I was bitten!’
I was bitten!'
After getting to the boy, Hammel says he noticed that his entire collarbone was torn open.
‘I shouted everyone to get out of the water: There’s a shark in the water!’ Hammel added
The boy was flown to Rady's children's hospital in San Diego, where his condition is said to be critical.
The type of shark that attacked is not known.
Life-saving captain Larry Giles said at a media conference that a shark had been spotted in the area a few weeks earlier, but it was found to be a harmless species.
Giles added that the victim sustained serious injuries to his upper body.
Authorities closed the beaches from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Ecinitas in Swam for 48 hours for investigative and safety reasons.
Giles pointed out that there are more than 135 sharks in the area, but most of them are considered harmless.
Sainsbury's is going to the UK beauty industry
Sainsbury's challenges Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams in department store-style beauty departments serving expert advisors.
This is a significant move for the UK's 2.8 billion pound beauty industry, which continues to grow, despite the decline in sales of fashion and houseware. Larger beauty departments are being tested in 11 stores across the country, and if successful, they will be extended to other stores next year.
Beauty care products are placed because supermarkets strive to fill the shelf space that had previously been reserved for TVs, microwave ovens and household items.
Sainsbury's says its range of beauty products doubles to 3 000 products, which for the first time include brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
Existing brands such as L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees will also get more space, and they'll get their own brand-specific areas like Boots stores.
In the future, the majority of products will be vegan-friendly, as it is increasingly demanded by young shoppers.
In addition, The Fragrance Shop perfume chain is experimenting with rental shops in two Sainsbury's stores. The first was opened last week in Croydon, South London, and the second will be opened in Selly Oaki in Birmingham later this year.
Internet shopping and the shift of grocery shopping to small one-time purchases in convenience stores forces supermarkets to come up with new incentives for customers.
Sainsbury's CEO Mike Coupe has said that stores will look even more like department stores as the supermarket chain struggles against low-cost stores like Aldi and Lidl by adding services and non-food products.
After buying both chains two years ago, Sainsbury's has opened hundreds of Argos stores in its stores, as well as Habitat stores. The company says this has contributed to the sale of food and made purchases worthwhile.
The supermarket failed in its previous attempt to renew its beauty and pharmacy departments.
Sainsbury's tried a joint venture with Boots in the early 2000's, but the cooperation ended in a dispute about how the proceeds of pharmacies located in supermarkets should be distributed.
The new strategy tracks the acquisition, in which Sainsbury's sold its 281-store pharmacy business to Celesio, which owns the Lloyds Pharmacy chain, for 125 million pounds three years ago.
The company said Lloyds is part of a plan to expand the range of luxury skincare products into four stores with brands such as La Roche-Posay and Vichy.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Chief Commercial Officer of Sainsbury, said: “We’ve changed the appearance and atmosphere of our beauty departments to improve the customer experience.
We have also invested in specially trained employees who are ready to advise customers.
Our range of brands meets all needs and our attractive surroundings and convenient locations make us an impressive beauty shopping destination that challenges the traditional way of shopping.”
