Hurricane Rosa brings the threat of heavy rain floods to Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City (drout zones may benefit)
Trophical low-pressures rarely arrive in Arizona, but that's exactly what's going to happen at the beginning of next week, when Hurricane Rosa's remnants move across the southwestern desert, bringing with it the risk of heavy rainfall.
The national weather service has already issued a warning of heavy rain floods over Monday and Tuesday from western Arizona to southern and eastern Nevada, southeastern Caifornia and Utah, as well as the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to advance directly across Phoenix on Tuesday, arriving on Monday in the form of rainfall.
Phoenix’s national weather service remarked in a tweet that “only 10 tropical hurricanes have retained a tropical storm or low-pressure space at a distance of 300 kilometers from Phoenix since 1950!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane at a distance of 65 kilometers from the Arizona border.”
The most recent modeling of the National Hurricane Center predicts rainfall of 50-100 millimetres. The Mogollin ridge can yield up to 150 millimetres in places.
In other areas of the southwestern desert, including the central rock mountains and Great Basin, 25-50 millilitres will probably be available. It can rain up to 100 millimetres in total.
Rosa's rains may also be a bless in an area affected by drought if there is no risk of heavy rain floods.
Although flooding poses a serious threat, some of the rains can be beneficial, as severe droughts have been suffered in the southern southwest.
According to the U.S. Drought Office, more than 40 percent of Arizona suffers from at least the extreme drought, which is the second-highest category, according to weather.com.
Hurricane Rosa will first make a landing on the California peninsula in Mexico.
On Sunday morning, Rosa's location is 620 kilometers south of Mexico's Punta Eugenia. It's still hurricane-strength at 135 km/hour. It's moving to the north at 20 km/hour.
The storm loses its power when confronted with cooler waters in the Pacific Ocean.
It is therefore expected to arrive in Mexico with a tropical turmoil during Monday afternoon or evening.
In parts of Mexico, the rains can be heavy and pose a significant risk of flooding.
‘From the California Peninsula to north-west Sonora, 75-150 millimetres are expected, and up to 250 millimetres is possible,’ says weather.com.
Rosa then continues her journey across Mexico as a tropical storm, before it arrives at the Arizona border as a tropical low-pressure on Tuesday morning hours. From there, it continues its journey through Arizona to southern Utah by the next night of Tuesday.
“The greatest risks of Rosa or its remnants are likely to relate to very heavy rain in the California Peninsula, northwestern Sonora, and the U.S. South-East Desert,” the National Hurricane Agency said.
These rains are expected to inflict life-threatening floods of heavy precipitation in the deserts, as well as landslides in mountainous areas.
