Hundreds of deaths in Indonesia earthquake and tsunami, the number of fatalities is expected to rise
Authorities said on Saturday at least 384 deaths after a major earthquake and tsunami hit Sulawes Island in Indonesia. Many were wiped by giant waves hitting the shores.
Hundreds of people had gathered for the festival on Friday, when waves of up to six meters high hit the beach at twilight. Many washed away their deaths as the waves destroyed everything.
The tsunami was caused by 7.5 magnitude earthquakes.
When the tsunami threat emerged yesterday, people went on the beach and didn’t immediately leave, and they were the victims,” said Purwo Nugroho, a spokesman for Indonesia’s BNPB.
‘The tsunami did not come alone, but drew cars, tree trunks, houses and struck all over the land,’ says Nugroho, adding that the tsunami reached 800 km/hour on the open sea before hitting the coast.
Some people survived the tsunami by climbing trees, he says.
Approximately 16,700 people were evacuated to 24 centers in Palu.
The aerial photographs released by the Disasteration Agency show a number of destroyed houses and shops, twisted and collapsed bridges, and a mosque surrounded by water.
Aftershocks further shook the coastal city on Saturday.
Earthquakes were felt in an area where 2.4 million people live.
BPPT, an agency for the evaluation and application of Indonesia's technology, issued a statement that the energy released in Friday's massive earthquake corresponded to about 200 atomic bombs that were knocked down during the Hiroshimaa Second World War.
It says that the terrain of a city at the end of a long and narrow bay may have inflated the tsunami.
Nugroho described the destruction as ‘extensive’ and said that thousands of houses, hospitals, shopping centers and hotels had collapsed.
