Nugroho said that the fatalities and that the damage might be greater from Palus 300 kilometers north along the ongoing coast, in an area called Donggala, which is closer to the center of the quake.
Communication connections “are completely exhausted and no information has been received from Donggala,” says Nugroho.
According to a statement from the Red Cross, more than 300 000 people live there, adding that its staff and volunteers were on their way to the area.
“It’s already a tragedy, but it can get a lot worse,” he said.
On Saturday, the agency was widely criticized for failing to say the tsunami had struck the return, although authorities said that the waves had arrived while the warning was being issued.
In an amateur video shared on social media, a man on the upper floors of the building zealously shouts warnings of an impending tsunami to people on the street.
Just minutes later, a wall of water hits the beach that takes away buildings and cars.
Reuters was not immediately able to verify the authenticity of the footage.
The Earthquake and tsunami caused an extensive power outage, which severed communications around Palu, which hampered the coordination of the authorities' rescue efforts.
Authorities say the army has begun sending aid from Jakarta and other cities with transport planes, but evacuees still badly need food and other basic supplies.
The city's airport has been reopened only for relief efforts and will remain closed until October.
President Either Widodo was scheduled to visit Palu evacuation centers on Sunday.
The Indonesian tsunami's death toll exceeds 800.
It's a really bad situation.
World Vision's Donggala staff has been able to take refuge in the city of Palu, where they have been accommodated in tent fabric shelters in the office's courtyard. On their way, they passed the scene of destruction, says Doseba.
“They told me they saw a lot of destroyed houses,” he said.
It's a really bad situation.
As aid teams embarked on their inconsolable mission, some complained that foreign aid workers were prevented from travelling to Return.
According to Indonesia's regulations, funding, supplies and personnel from abroad can only begin to flow once the disaster area has been declared a national disaster zone.
This hasn't happened yet.
‘It’s still a provincial disaster,’ said Aulia Arriani, a spokesman for Indonesia’s Red Cross.
‘As long as the government says ‘OK, it’s a national disaster,’ we can start receiving international assistance, but the declaration hasn’t been made yet.”
When the second night descended over Palu after the Friday earthquake and tsunami, the friends and family members of the missing lived the hope that their loved ones would miraculously deviate from the dark story of natural disasters.
On Saturday, a little boy was lifted from the sewer.
On Sunday, rescue workers released a woman who had been in rubble for two days next to her mother's body.
