Corporations should double funding for artificial intelligence research
The U.S. should double its funding to study artificial intelligence as China intensifies its activities in the industry, says Kai-Fu Lee, a financier and artificial intelligence professional who worked at Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The statement was preceded by communications on the artificial intelligence in various parts of the U.S. administration, although the United States lacks a formal AI strategy.
In the meantime, China presented its plans last year: it aims to become a leading developer of artificial intelligence by 2030.
“Doubling the budget for artificial intelligence research would be a good start, considering that other countries are so far behind the United States, and that we are looking for the next breakthrough in artificial intelligence,” says Lee.
A doubling of the funding could double the likelihood that the next major AI achievement would take place in the United States, Lee said in a CNBC interview this week.
Lee’s book “Great Powers of Artificial Intelligence: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month through Houghton Miffli’s Harcourt publishing house. He works as CEO of Sinovation Venture, who has invested in one of China’s most prominent artificial intelligence companies called Face+.
In the 1980's, he developed an artificial intelligence system at Carnegie Mellon University, which defeated Othello, the highest-ranked Othello player in the United States. He later held senior positions at Microsoft Research, as well as the head of Google's operations in China.
Lee recognized the U.S. administration's previous technology competitions, such as the Defense Advanced Research Projects and the Agency's Robotics Challenge, and asked when the next one would be held to identify future visionaries.
Lee says U.S. investigators worked hard to get a public grant.
‘Academic influencers aren’t taken by China, but by corporations,’ Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have recruited top universities to work with artificial intelligence.
Lee also said immigration policy changes could help the United States to intensify their AI efforts.
‘In my opinion, those who have a doctoral degree in artificial intelligence should be automatically awarded green cards,’ he said.
China's State Treasury its next-generation AI development plan in July 2017.
The Chinese National Natural Sciences Foundation provides funding to people working in academic institutions in the same way as the National Science Foundation and other state-owned organizations allocate money to U.S. researchers, but the quality of academic work is lower in China, Lee said.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defensity established the Defense Department Joint Artificial Intelligence Center to bring together partners in the industry and the academic world. The White House has also announced the formation of a special committee on artificial intelligence.
DARPA also announced an investment of US$2 billion this month for an initiative called AI Next.
NSF, on the other hand, invests more than 100 million dollars annually in artificial intelligence research.
At the same time, the bill aimed at setting up the National Security Commission on AI has not progressed for months.
Macedonians in a referendum to change the country's name
In Sunday’s referendum, Macedonians voted to change the country’s name ‘Northern Macedonian Republic’. The name change would resolve a decades-long dispute with Greece, which has blocked the country’s aspirations to become a member of the European Union and NATO.
According to Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, the northern neighbour's name is, in practice, a regional requirement for the territory that belongs to it, and has therefore objected to its membership of the EU and NATO.
In June, the governments of the countries signed an agreement on a new name proposal, but nationalist opponents felt that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's Slavic majority.
President Gjorge Ivanov has announced that he does not intend to vote in a referendum, and the anti-voting campaign raises doubts as to whether voter turnout will reach the required 50 per cent in order for the result to be legal.
The question on the ballot was: ‘Are you on the side of NATO and EU membership, accepting the agreement with Greece?’
Proponents of a name change such as Prime Minister Zoran Zaevi reason that it is worth paying the price in order for Macedonia, which has emerged from the collapse of Yugoslavia, to seek membership of both the EU and NATO. . .
“Today I came to vote for the future of our country, on behalf of the youth of Macedonia, so that they can live in freedom as members of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us,” 79-year-old Olivera Georgievska said.
Although voting is not legally binding, a sufficient number of MEPs have said that they respect the outcome of the vote in order to have a decisive effect.
A two-thire majority in Parliament will be required for a name change.
According to the Central Election Committee, no irregularities had been reported by 13 o'clock.
However, the turnout was only 16, compared to 34 per cent in the previous parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 per cent of those entitled to vote voted.
‘I came to vote for my children and our place in Europe,’ said 62-year-old Gjose Tanevski, who voted in the capital Skopje.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and son Dushko voted in a referendum on Macedonia's name change in Strumica, Macedonia, on 30 September 2018. The name change opened the way for NATO and EU membership .
54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov was preparing a small pallet and placing chairs in front of tents boycotted in front of Skopje's parliament.
“We’re in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to join with our heads up and not through the kitchen door,” Kavadarkov said.
“We’re a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don’t want to take us as Macedonia, we can turn elsewhere, such as China and Russia as part of the euro-asian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev said NATO membership will bring much-needed investment in Macedonia with an unemployment rate of more than 20%.
“I believe a large majority will vote in favour of a name change because more than 80% of our citizens are in favour of the EU and NATO,” Zaev said.
He said “yes” the voice is “our ‘preparing future.’”
An opinion poll published on Monday by the Political Research Institute in Macedonia referred to the turnout of 30-43 per cent in a referendum - that is, below the required activity.
In another survey commissioned by Telma TV in Macedonia, 57% of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
70% of them said they would vote in favour of the ‘‘yes’ option.
In order for the referendum to succeed, the voter turnout must exceed 50% by one vote.
Failure of the referendum would be the first serious setback for a pro-Western government when it came to power in May last year.
