Labour is no longer a tolerant church.
It doesn't tolerate those who say what they think
I wasn't really surprised when the Momentum activists of my local party supported a vote of censure against me.
After all, I'm only the last Labour representative in the queue, who is told I'm not welcome - just because we're saying our opinion.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan was treated in the same way just because he strongly opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case, the motion of censure was a criticism that I disagreed with Jeremy Corbyn.
On issues relating to responsible economic policy, national security, Europe, ironically similar topics that Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
Nottingham East’s Labour Conference stated on Friday that “we want our meetings to be inclusive and productive.”
Most of the eight years I've served as a local Labour representative, that's exactly what the party board's Friday-sizes have been.
Now, not many meetings follow that tone of voice, and ‘friendlier and gentler’ politics have long since been overlooked if it ever began.
It is increasingly clear that the Labour Party will no longer tolerate differing opinions, and that each opinion will be judged on the basis of whether it is suitable for the party leadership or not.
This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader, and colleagues with whom they thought I would share a similar political view began to look forward to a turnaround from me and statements that I otherwise would never have consented to, whether it was national security or the EU's internal market.
Every time I speak publicly - it doesn't really matter what I'm saying - the result is an avalanche of insults on social media calling for the cancellation of my election, the rejection of centrist politics, and I'll hear that I shouldn't be in the Labour Party.
And it's not just my own experience.
Indeed, I know that I am more fortunate than some of my colleagues, because at least the comments directed at me are political in nature.
I have great respect for the professionalism and determination of my colleagues, who face sexist and racist abuse on a daily basis and never wavers.
The normalization of maltreatment is one of the most disturbing features in the politics of this era.
Last week, Jeremy Corbyn insisted that the Labour Party should adopt a culture of tolerance.
In reality, we are no longer a party that accepts different opinions, and with every motion of censure or a change in confidence, the party will only become more limited.
For two years, I’ve been urged to keep a low profile and refrain from expressing my thoughts so that “everything goes well.”
But that's not the reason I went into politics.
Ever since I joined the Labour Party as a student 32 years ago as a result of the Omission of the Thatcher Government's Comprehensive School, both as a city councillor and as a minister, I have tried to promote better public services for those who need them most.
I've never covered my political line, not even in the last election.
No one in the constituency of Nottingham East has been surprised by my perspectives or disagreements with the current leadership.
For those who advocated the initiative last Friday, I would like to say that as the state progresses towards Brexit, which harms households, businesses and public services, I do not understand the need to waste time and energy on questioninging my confidence in the leadership of the Labour Party.
But my true message is not to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents: I represent you with honor, and the threat of dismissal or the promotion of a political career does not stop me from acting in a way that I think is in your interests.
Chris Leslie is a Member of Parliament from Nottingham East constituency
Ayr - Melrose 38-17: Undefeated Ayr on the top
Two last-minute goals slightly distorted the outcome of the match, but Ayr undeniably deserved a win in today's entertaining Tennent ́s Premiership.
Ayr is now at the top of the table as the only team out of ten that hasn't lost any matches.
A great defense and a more successful risk-taking was, after all, what supported them, and coach Peter Murchie could be pleased for a reason.
“We’ve been challenged in our matches and we’re still undefeated, so I can be satisfied.”
Melrosen Robyn Christie says, “All credit to Ayr, they took risks better than we did.”
Ayr took the lead in 14 minutes with Grant Anderson's goal and Frazier Climo's successful conversion-kick, but Scotland captain Roy Hughes' yellow card allowed Melrose to rise, and Jason Baggot scored.
Climo increased Ayr's lead with a penalty goal, and just before half-time, he scored and managed a conversion-kick, increasing Ayri's lead to 17-5.
But Melrose started the second period well with Patrick Anderson's goal, whose conversion-kick Baggot managed to narrow the gap to five points.
There was a long delay in the match when Ruaridh Knott was seriously injured and carried off the field. As the game resumed, Ayr increased his lead with Stafford McDowall's goal, which Climo was able to score.
Ayr's captain Blair Macpherson received a yellow card, followed by Melrose's Bruce Colvine, after intense pressure, scored a conversion-kick.
However, the home team responded when Struan Hutchinson received a yellow card for tackling the ballless Climo, and Macpherson scored from the side-threshot behind Ayr's mail.
Climo did the conversion kick the same way as before, and Kyle Rowe snatched David Armstrong's kick and swung Gregor Henry to complete the home team's fifth goal.
The star of the Still Game series begins in the restaurant industry
Ford Kieran, the star of the Still Game series, has been nominated as the director of a licensed restaurant company and is likely to start working in the restaurant industry as well.
The 56-year-old actor plays Jack Jarvis on a popular BBC show he writes with comedy partner Greg Hemphill.
The duo has announced that the ninth season will be the last in the series, and it seems that Kiernan is already planning his life after Craiglang.
According to public register records, he has been registered as a director at Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor himself declined to comment on the information, but a source of the Scottish Sun newspaper hinted that Kiernan wanted to be involved in Glasgow’s thriving restaurant business.
‘The sea belongs to us’: Landlocked Bolivia is trying to regain a connection to the Pacific Ocean through justice
Sailors patrol outside a naval base in La Paz.
There's a sea-blue flag on the flagpoles of public buildings.
Fleet bases from Titicaca to the Amazon have been smeared with the slogan: “Fairly, the sea belongs to us.
Getting it back is our duty.”
In inland Bolivia, the memory of the shoreline lost to Chile in a bloody 19th-century Resource War is still strong – as is the longing to sail once again in the Pacific Ocean.
Hope is at its highest in decades as Bolivia awaits the decision of the International Court of Justice on October 1, after five years of reflection.
‘Bolivia has a strong belief in progress, team spirit and serenity, and, of course, a positive attitude towards waiting for the final solution,’ said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Several Bolivians around the country are hopefully following the decision of the International Court of Justice on the big screens, hopining that the Hague will, at the end of a decades-long debate, support Bolivia's position that Chile has an obligation to grant Bolivia's independent access to the sea.
Bolivia's charismatic president Evo Morales, who will face a controversial re-election next year, will have a lot at stake in next Monday's policy.
‘The return to the Pacific Ocean is very close,’ he said at the end of August.
However, some of the analysts believe that the court is unlikely to decide in Bolivia's best interests, and even if it did, the situation would hardly change.
In addition, ICJ has announced that it will not determine the outcome of any negotiations.
The fact that the ICJ's decision is issued just six months after the last contributions suggests that the case ‘isn’t complicated,’ says Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
In addition, for the past four years have slowed down rather than promoting Bolivia's intentions.
‘The current Bolivian government has abducted the question of maritime contact,” said Zárate.
Morales' quarreling rhetoric has suffocated the rest of Chilean benevolence, he said.
Bolivia and Chile will continue the talks at some point, but it will be very difficult to organise negotiations after that.
There hasn't been another embassy in either country since 1962.
Former President and Bolivia's spokesman in The Hague, Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, does not accept the view that the court's decision would have been exceptionally swift.
Monday gives Bolivia “an exceptional opportunity to embark on a new era in relations with Chile” and an opportunity “to “end 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefits,” he said.
Calzadilla also denies that Morales, one of Latin America's most popular presidents, used the maritime issue as political support.
‘Bolivia will never hand over her right of connection to the Pacific Ocean,’ she added.
“The decision is an opportunity to understand that it’s time for us to move on from the past.”
North Korea announced that denuclearization would not take place unless it can trust the United States
“North Korea won’t give up their nuclear weapons first unless they can rely on Washington,” North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho said.
Ri spoke at the UN General Assembly on Saturday.
He reminded the United States to keep the promises made by the Heads of State at the Singapore Summit.
Ri's statement came just before U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo is about to regainize deadlock diplomacy with nuclear weapons, just three months after the U.S. meet Kim Jong Un in Singapore.
Ri said it was a “distant dream” to believe that extended sanctions and U.S. opposition to the proclamation of the end of the Korean War would bring North Korea to its knees.
Until Pyongyang has first taken important measures to promote disarmament, Washington is on alert to the adoption of the proclamation.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want another summit.
But the view that Pyongyang was about to give up an arsenal of weapons, which the state probably sees as the only real means of guaranteeing its own safety has received widespread suspicion.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for the next Kim-Trump summit.
The Paris fashion shows will present the large headgears of the latest collection, which will soon also be on your nearby street
If you want to enlarge your hat collection or cover the sun completely, you don't have to keep looking.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne unveiled at their SS19 collection show a selection of peculiar over-the-top headwear that dazzled the Paris Fashion Week styles.
Very impractical headgears took over Instagram this summer, and these designers have sent their amazing creations to fashion shows as well.
Valentino's most eye-catching creation was a beige hat with a wide feather brim, which covered the ends of the models almost completely.
Other oversized accessories included watermelon decorated with jewels, a wizard's hat and even pineapple – but they're not designed to keep your head warm.
Thom Browne also unveiled a selection of peculiar masks – just in time for Halloween.
Several colorful masks had sewn their lips, and they looked more like Hannibal Lecter than high fashion.
One creation with a snorkel and a mask looked more like a diving tool, and the other resembled a melted ice cream cone.
And if you want to take a stand on behalf of big fashion, you're lucky.
Fashion followers predict that huge scarves are about to appear on the street scene.
Exaggerated hats closely follow “La Bomba”, a half-meter brimmed straw hat that has been seen by everyone from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult fame of an impractical hat that appeared on social media also inspired another big creation for fashion shows – a beach bag made of straw, which is almost as large as the model that carries it.
A burnt orange reed reed bag with white leather hand straps was the most eye-catching creation from Jacquemus' La Riviera's SS19 collection at Paris Fashion Week.
Famous stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “I expect to see bulky hats and beach bags in the street scene next summer – the designers have made such a big impression that it would be difficult to ignore the demand for large accessories.”
John Edward: language skills are a key skill for global citizens
The learning outcomes of private schools in Scotland have traditionally been good, and this success has also continued in 2018 as a result of good test results. Success has been enhanced by individual and communal success in sports, arts, music and other communal projects.
These schools of more than 30 000 pupils throughout Scotland, represented by SCIS, are represented by the SCIS, which strives to provide a high level of service to their pupils as well as to the parents of the pupils.
Private schools strive to prepare their students for their chosen careers, further and higher education, as well as for global citizenship.
As an educational operator that can design and implement tailor-made study content, we have noticed the status of languages as a popular and desirable subject to continue in educational institutions.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you’re speaking to a person in a language that he understands, the message goes into his head.
If you speak to a person in their own language, the message goes into their heart.”
This is a strong reminder that we cannot rely only on the English language if we want to create relationships with the people of other countries.
From this year's most recent test results, we can see that languages are at the top of the charts in terms of the entry rate between private schools.
68 percent of students who studied foreign languages achieved the highest grade of A.
Based on the data, it was found that pupils who received the best grade A, 72 per cent studied Mandarin, 72 per cent studied German, 69 per cent studied French and 63 per cent studied Spanish.
This shows that private schools in Scotland support the study of foreign languages as an essential skill that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages are currently optional subjects, but they are valued at the same rate as natural sciences (science, technology, engineering and mathematics), both in the study programme of private schools and elsewhere.
According to a survey commissioned by the UK Employment Office in 2014, it was found that 17 per cent of situations in which the employer had difficulty finding a suitable employee for the job were explained with inadequate language skills.
Therefore, the development of language skills is even more necessary in terms of promoting the future career development of young people.
The more possible tasks require language skills, the more relevant these skills will become in the global world.
If a person has studied another language, they will have an advantage in the future from this lifelong skill, regardless of the career they have chosen.
The ability to communicate directly with a foreigner automatically puts a multilingual person ahead in the competition.
According to a survey commissioned by YouGov in 2013, 75 per cent of more than 4,000 Iso-British adults are unable to engage in discussion in a foreign language, and French was the only two-digit number 15 per cent.
That is why investing in the teaching of languages is now important for today's children.
In particular, knowledge of the languages of developing countries gives children a better opportunity to find a meaningful career.
Within Scotland, schools differ in what languages they teach.
Several schools focus on traditional languages, while others specifically teach languages that supposedly are important to Iso-Britans in 2020, such as Mandarin and Japanese.
Despite your child's interests, private schools always have several language options taught by specialized teachers.
The private schools in Scotland are dedicated to providing a learning environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills needed to succeed, whatever the future holds.
At this time, it cannot be denied that, in an international business environment, languages will continue to be very important for the future of the country, so this must be reflected in education.
Indeed, modern languages should be seen as ‘international communication skills’.
Private schools will continue to offer this freedom of choice, versatility and a high-level for young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is the head of SCIS of the Scottish Private School Council
