Trump's West Virginia campaign time, YouTube channel
President Donald Trump will begin a series of election campaigns today in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It's Trump's first of the five election events scheduled for next week, including sympathetic targets such as Tennesee and Mississippi.
Waiting for the vote, while awaiting his vote to fill the Supreme Court's vacancy, Trump is seeking to increase support for the upcoming midterm elections, as Republicans are in danger of losing a majority in congression in November.
At what time is Trump's West Virginia campaign today and how do you see it online?
Trump's West-Virginian Wheeling event starts at 19 o'clock on the East Coast on Saturday 29.9.2018.
You can watch Trump's West Virginia campaign event online as live streaming through YouTube by clicking below.
Trump is likely to comment on this week's Supreme Court's Chief Judiciary Candidate Brett Kavanaugh, who became fervent over sexual harassment allegations that have delayed the Senate Confirmation Vote as much as a week in the FBI's Investigation.
But the primary goal for a series of campaigns is to support Republican candidates' success in a tight November election.
Accordingly, President Trump’s campaign commented on these next week’s five event is to “awaken volunteer and profitable Republicans to hedage and expand Majorities in the Senate and the House of Representatives,” according to Reuters.
“Congress management is so important to him that the president is willing to travel to as many states as possible during a busy campaign period,” a spokesman who stood anonymously for the Trump campaign told Reuters.
Today’s venue is Wesbanco Arena Wheeling, and supporters can even arrive “From Ohio and Pennsylvania, as well as media representatives from Pittsburgh,” according to West Virginia Metro News.
Saturday is Trump's second visit this month in West Virginia, a state where he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump supports the Republican West Virginia Senatorial Candidate Patrick Morrisey, who has lagged behind in the polls.
“From Morrisey’s point of view, it’s not a good sign that the president is trying to increase his support in opinion polls,” said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of Western Virginia.
Ryder Cup 2018: The U.S. team demonstrates the will to keep hope alive before Sunday's single-playout
After three one-sided sessions, Saturday afternoon doubles were exactly what this Ryder Cup needed.
Although the self-reinforcing power of success is a fully invented phenomenon in sports, players still believe in it, and even more in tournaments like this.
According to them, where is this power of success at the moment?
“They had a six-point lead that narrowed to four, so we’re going to take this success,” Jordan Spieth said as he walked away at the end of the day.
Europe has, of course, an advantage: four points ahead and 12 to play.
The Americans, as Spieth said, feel they're getting a bit of wind in their sails and a lot to be encouraged about, such as Spieth's and Justin Thomas' condition, with which they played together all day and scored three points out of four.
Spieth has been superior from the brick to the green, and leads by example.
The hoarse throats of the triumph were intensified as he progressed as he progressed in a critical putt to equalize the match to exactly four when he and Thomas were two points behind after two holes.
Putta, with which they won the 15th match was accompanied by a similar cry, which gave the impression that he believes that the U.S. team has not yet lost.
“You need to focus really well and only take care of your own match,” Spieth said.
It's the only thing these players have left.
18 holes to impress.
The only players who have scored more points than Spieth and Thomas in the last two days are the undisputed legends Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood of the Ryder Cup.
Europe's peculiar but sweet duo has won four out of four flawlessly.
‘Moliwood’ was the only duo who didn’t make any bogs on Saturday afternoon, but they avoided bogeys also on Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and the last nine holes on Friday morning.
That round, and the way they're giving and getting energy from this raucous crowd confirms that they're Sunday's early favorites. And as the sun sets at Le Golf National, there couldn't be another more popular player to secure a possible victory in Europe than Fleetwood and Molinari.
Preferably both at the same time with different holes.
However, talk of Europe's success is premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson quickly defeated Sergio Garcia, who was the hero of the morning's doubles with Alex Noren.
One bogi and two double bogs on the first nine holes dug the Spaniard and the Swede so deep into the hole that they weren't anywhere near surviving.
However, on Sunday, no one will come to help you out of the hole.
The four-ball and doubles are fascinating to follow closely from the point of view of couples' collaborations: What kind of advice do they give or don't give and how the strategy can change in no time.
Europe has played better as a team so far and took a prominent lead before the last day, but this doubles match showed that the U.S. team has the will to fight, even though it has been suspected, especially in the United States.
Europe will take a 10-6 lead ahead of Ryder Cup's final day
Europe will be in a good lead before the Ryder Cup's final day after taking a 10-6 lead in Saturday's quadruples and doubles from the United States.
Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led a two-win triumph over Tiger Woods and got a high score to four points today at Le Golf National.
Thomas Bjorn's European side, who made a great effort to maintain two years in Hazeltine's win, dominated the poorly-played U.S. team in four-ballon in the morning, taking the match 3-1.
The United States were a tougher opponent in doubles, winning two matches, but they were unable to narrow the lead.
Jim Furyk's half needed eight points out of Sunday's 12 singles to maintain the win.
Fleetwood is the first European beginner to win four points in a row. He and Molinari, also known as “Molliwood” together, were the second duo in the history of the Ryder Cup, winning four points in the first found.
After crushing Woods and Patrick Reed in a four-ball, they then scored a 5-4 lead to a 5-4 finish between the discouraged Woods and Bryson Dechambeau.
Woods, who barely made it through Saturday's two games, was very brilliant at times, but has nevertheless lost 19 of his 29 games in four-ballon and doubles, and even seven in a row.
Justin Rose rested his morning four-balls, but returned with Henrik Stenson to doubles, but lost 2-1 to Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, who are first and third in the rankings.
Europe didn't get everything it wanted on this fresh and pleasing day southwest from Paris.
Three-time title winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas set a high goal for the U.S. with two points on Saturday.
They got a 2-1 win over Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter of Spain in a four-power, and later a 4-3 victory over Poulter and Rory McIlroy after losing the first two holes.
Only twice in the history of the Ryder Cup, the team has risen from a four-point chase to level with the start of doubles, although on Furyk's side a draw is also enough to maintain the victory.
However, after two days of being the second best, it may be that Sunday's counter-attack is too much to ask for.
North Korea ‘by no means’ disarms itself without confidence
North Korea's foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that continued sanctions only increased distrust to the United States, and under these circumstances there is no chance that North Korea would unilaterally give up its nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho told the U.N. General Assembly that over the past year, North Korea has taken “significant benevolent measures,” such as stopping nuclear and missile tests, dismantling a test site for nuclear tests, and promising that North Korea will not increase the amount of nuclear weapons or technology.
‘However, we have not seen such a gesture from the United States,’ he said.
“Without confidence in the United States, we cannot be sure of the preservation of national security, so in such circumstances it is not possible for us to disarm ourselves unilaterally.”
Although Ri reiterated a familiar hymn that Washington does not accept the step-by-step abolition of nuclear weapons in which North Korea would be rewarded for every subjugation, his statement is nevertheless significant in the sense that unilateral disarmament was not completely ruled out, as Pyongyang has done in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement that Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump gave at the first U.S. sitting president's and North Korea's leader's summition in Singapore on 12.6
North Korea has attempted to officially end the 1950–1953 Korean War, but the United States has demanded that Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also objected to easing strict international sanctions imposed on North Korea.
“The U.S. demanded ‘nuclear weapons removal first,’ and they’ve been under pressure through sanctions to forcibly achieve their goals—even against the ‘peace declaration’,” Ri said.
“The idea that sanctions would bring us to our knees is the desire of the indifferent.
But the problem is that constant sanctions only deepen our distrust.”
Ri didn't mention plans to hold the next Kim-Trump summit, which Trump himself emphasized earlier this week.
On the other hand, the minister emphasized three meetings with South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months and added: “If the party demanding disarmament were South Korea and not the United States, the situation would not have reached such an impasse.”
Still, Ri’s tone of voice was significantly different from last year when he told the UN General Assembly that targeting missiles towards the U.S. continentaries was inevitable after “Evil President” Trump called Kim a “missiller” armed with suicidal intent.
As recently as last year, Trump threatened to ‘destroy’ North Korea. However, this year, Trump praised Kimi profusely for his courage to approach the renunciation of weapons, but still emphasized that there is still a lot of work to be done and the sanctions must remain as long as North Korea renounces nuclear weapons.
On Wednesday, Trump mentioned that he has no temporal deadline: “If it takes two years, three years or five months, it doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia argued that the UN Security Council should reward Pyongyang for advances that have already been made.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday that ‘Confirmation of Security Council Sanctitions must remain strong and unsuccessful until we testify the full, final, and confirmed abolition of nuclear weapons.’
The Security Council has unanimously intensified sanctions against North Korea since 2006 with the aim of stagnating the funding channels for the production of Pyongyang's ballistic and nuclear missiles.
Pompeo met with North Korea's Rin during the UN General Assembly. He also announced that he would visit Pyongyang again next month for preparations for the second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but the last visit did not go smoothly.
After leaving Pyongyang, he described the progress. However, North Korea condemned Pompeo's "gangster-like demands" just a few hours later.
North Korea undertooked the closure of a missile base and nuclear facility during a meeting with Secretary-General Moon earlier this month if the U.S. is conducting “equivalent measures.”
According to Pompeo, Kim means ‘equivalent measures’ the security guarantees Trump committed to during the Singapore meeting and the transition towards the normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard students will complete an instructive course for adequate rest
All undergraduate students at Harvard University are attending a course this year aimed at increasing student rest. The course is a backlash against the macho culture thriving at the university, where students work a long night with the power of caffeine.
According to the researcher, students of the world's best university are often quite unaware of the basics related to their own well-being.
Charles Czeisler, a professor at Harvard University’s Faculty of Medicine who is working on sleep research at Brigham and Women’s Hospital, believes the course will be the first of its kind in the United States.
Czeisler got the idea for the course after giving a lecture on the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
‘After the lecture, a young woman came to me and said, ‘What should I hear about it now, in my last year of college?’
No one had ever told him the necessity to sleep, which I thought was surprising,” he said in an interview with the Telegraph.
Organised for the first time this year, the course will guide students on how the right sleep habits support academic and athletic performance and improve overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, a professor at Harvard University's Faculty of Medicine and deputy head of the university's health services, says the university ended up running a course after hearing students suffered from severe sleep deprivations during the study week.
The one-hour course includes a number of interactive assignments.
In one section, pupils are presented with a picture of a dormitory, where the student can click on coffee cups, curtains, sports equipment and books, and thus get information about the effects of caffeine and light, as well as the impact of sleep deprivation on sports performance and the importance of regular evening activities.
In the second section, the participants will learn about how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risks of a heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
The campus map with interactive icons encourages participants to reflect on their daily routines.
“We know that this has no immediate impact on student behavior.
However, we believe that they have the right to know about these issues in exactly the same way that they have the right to know about the health effects of smoking,” adds Professor Czeisler.
According to Professor Czeisler, study marathons that last all night are still a topic of pride, and modern technology and the ever-increasing pressures of students made sleep deprivation a growing problem.
‘Getting adequate and high-quality sleep should be a ‘secret weapon’ of students against stress, exhaustion and anxiety, and even against weight gain, because sleep deprivation causes a state that resembles starvation in the brain, which is why a person is constantly hungry,” the professor points out.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student of chemical and physical psychology in California, helped Professor Czeisler plan the course after attending one of the courses he had taught in his first year at Harvard last year.
He says that the course opened his eyes and pusted all the students on the campus to organize a comprehensive course.
He hopes that all doctoral students will be able to complete a similar study module before moving on to a competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler recommends that students send an alarm clock to also remind students of bedtime in addition to waking up in the morning, as well as prompting them to keep in mind the adverse effects of electric display devices and “blue light” in LED lighting on their circadian rhythm, which can make it difficult to fall asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga's Shot Won by Gerrard
Steven Gerrard and the Rangers experienced yet another losing day when Dolly Menga's shot through the defensive brought Livingston a 1–0 win.
Ibrox's players were claiming their first win since February's 4–1 game against St. Johnstone. However, Gary Holt's team won this time, and Gerrard just had his second loss during his 18-game manager's career. The Rangers were eight points away from the Premier League's current leader, Hearts.
Menga shot the ball to the goal seven minutes before half-time, and the Rangers didn't even the situation any further.
As a result of the game, the Rangers fell to sixth place. Livingston, on the other hand, has climbed to third rank, with only Hibernian in the goal difference.
Rangers may be facing other problems, as Calum Spence was injured during the game when an object throwned out of the auditorium hit the head, and the wound had to be treated.
Gerrard made eight substitutions to the line-up, with which the team had beaten Ayri and advanced to the semi-finals of the Scottish league cup.
Holt continued with the same Livi 11 line-up, where the team took a point from the Hearts last week. The same strategy again paid off when the well-constructed line-up managed to discipline the opponent at almost every turn.
Although the Rangers managed to dominate the possession of the ball, Livingston was better to use the situations to their own advantage.
Livingston had the chance to score in just two minutes when Menga's first serve gave Scott Pittman a free way to Allan McGregor's goal, but Pittman failed to take advantage of the opportunity.
Keaghan Jacobs then directed his free-kick to Craig Halkett, but defenseman Alan Lithgow only managed to shoot broadly into the back post.
The Rangers, on the one hand, took control of the game, but in the last third of the game there was no more self-confidence in the air.
Alfredo Morelos demanded a penalty for fifteen minutes when he and Steven Lawless collided with each other, but Judge Steven Thomson ignored the Colombian's requests.
The Rangers attempted only two goals in the first half, but former Ibrox goaltender Liam Kelly effortlessly blocked Lassana Coulibaly's butt and Ovie Ejaria's powerless shot.
Livingston's goal at 34 minutes was an unexpected opening, but it wasn't just the team's hard work.
The Rangers still couldn't do anything for Jacob's long passing tactics.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher fed Scott Robinson, who cold-headededly escorted the ball to Menga for an easy goal.
At the time of the exchange, Gerrard decided to replace Coulibaly and put Ryan Kent on the field. The exchange affected the game almost immediately when Kent created a suitable gap for Morelos. However, the attack snapped into Kelly's impressively fast defense.
Livingston made the visitors play their own game again as Lithgow and Halkett resumed long assists one after the other.
Holt's team had the opportunity to elevate their lead in the final moments of the game, but McGregor's fine defense blocked Jacobs' plans before Lithgow had time to get into his position in the corner.
Glenn Middleton, who came out of the Rangers' exchangation, also demanded a penalty after clashing with Jacobs, but Thomson again looked away.
Almanaca: Geiger meter inventor
Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger, a future physicist, was born in Germany 136 years ago.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity. The invention later led to the development of a device known as a geiger meter.
The method became one of the pillars of science, and the key concept of pop culture. For example, the 1950 film “Coronado Watches”, in which Roy Rogers and Dale Evans portray scientists who seem quite like cowboys:
Man: “What on earth is that?”
Rogers: “It’s a geiger meter. It’s used to find radioactive minerals such as uranium.
When you put your headphones on, you can actually hear the effect of radioactive radiation on minerals.”
Evans: ‘Look, it’s really cracking!’
“Hans” Geiger died in 19445 a few days before his 63rd birthday.
However, an invention that bears his name still lives on.
A new cancer vaccine is able to teach the immune system ‘to see’ malignant cells
The new cancer vaccine is capable of teaching the immune system to ‘sees’ malignant cells and destroy them
Vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells as part of treatment
In the method, resistant cells are isolated from the patient and altered in the laboratory
After treatment, cells injected back into the body are able to ‘see’ proteins, which are typical for a number of eaters
Thorotherapy has given promising results in patients with different cancers.
For example, the cancer cells of a patient with ovarian cancer disappeared within 18 months after being treated with a vaccine that teaches the immune system to identify malignant cells.
In the method, resistant cells are isolated from the patient and altered in the laboratory so that they are able to ‘see’ HER2 proteins that are typical for many cancers. The modified cells are finally injected back into the body.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda describes the method: ‘Based on our results, this is a very promising vaccine.’
HER2 ‘guides the growth of several types of cancer,’ Professor Berzofsky explains. “These include breast cancer as well as cancers of the ovaries, lungs, and colon.”
A similar approach – taking resistant cells from a patient and teaching them to attack cancer cells – has also produced results in the treatment of a type of leukaemia.
Kanye West got into the teeth of critics after acclading Trump on Saturday Night Live with a campaign hat in his head.
The performance didn't go as planned
Kanye West received boos on the Saturday Night Live entertainment program after acclaiming President Donald Trump in a flashy style and announcing that he was running for president in the 2020 election.
After presenting his third song of the evening “Ghost Town” in Trump’s Make America Great Again campaign hat, West attacked the democrats and reiterated his support for President Trump.
“Often when I’m having a conversation with a white person, they ask me how I can support Trump because he’s racist.
If racism were a problem, I would have moved from America a long time ago,” he said.
Matt Damon mocked Brett Kavanaughugh in the Senate Judicial Committee. The hearing relates to Christine Blasey Ford's allegations of sexual harassment by Kavanaugh.
West's ranting wasn't broadcast live, but comedian Chris Rock uploaded a snippet to social media.
It's unclear whether Rock intends to mock West with his post.
West also complained to the public that he had been abused behind the scenes due to the hat he was wearing.
“I was bullied behind the scenes.
I was told I shouldn't go on stage with this hat in my head.
I've been bullied!
“Then they’ll say I’m marginalized,” West said in an interview with Washington Examiner.
West continues: “Do you want to see what it means to be on the margins?” and says he’s dressing in Superman’s cloak, because ‘then you can’t prescribe what I’m doing. Do you want the world to move forward?
“Try with love.”
According to Variety, West’s comments prompted the audience at least twice, and the performers of the show looked embarrassed. One of those present described the situation to the newspaper as follows: “The whole studio went completely silent.”
West was selected on short notice to replace Ariana Grande, whose ex-boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West caused confusion in many viewers by performing the song “I love it” dressed as Perrier.
West receives support from Candace Turner, the leader of Conservative TPUSA, who praised his courage on Twitter: “THANK YOU FOR DEFYING TROUBLETES.”
Karen Hunter tweeted, however, that West “is just himself, which is absolutely wonderful.”
‘However, I may choose not to SUPport a person (building their music or clothing brand, or by supporting their ‘art’), which I consider to support and disseminate an ideology that is harmful to my community.
He's a free person.
The same goes for us,” he added.
The rapper announced on Twitter that he had changed his name to ‘Kanye West.’
West isn't the first artist to change his name. The musician here follows Diddy, also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
Another rapper, Snoop Dogg, was also known as Snoop Lion, and of course, we also remember the late music legend Prince, who switched his name to a symbol and then an artist who used to be known as Prince.
Belfast restaurant stabber accused of attempted murder
The 45-year-old man has been accused of attempted murder after he was stabbed on Friday at a restaurant in eastern Belfast.
According to police, the incident took place in the Ballyhackamore area.
The accused is expected to appear before a Belfastian court on Monday.
The charge will be checked by the Public Prosecutor's Office.
Kit Harington attacks toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his sword-waving heroic role as Jon Snow in HBO’s Violent Thread Game.
Harrington, however, has invaded the macho hero's stereotype: according to the 31-year-old actor, such roles give young boys the feeling that respect must be earned with hardness.
In an interview with the Sunday Times cultural supplement, Harrington suspects “something has gone wrong” in the matter, and questions the means in place to tackle toxic masculinity in the era of the #MeToo phenomenon.
Harrington, who has also recently married 31-year-old Rose Leslie, who is also a member of the Throsen Game, admits that he has a strong desire to address the subject.
“The question of where we’ve gone wrong with masculinity arouses strong sensations in me at the moment,” he says.
“What has men been taught in the years of their growth, which has led to problems that are now noticeable?”
Harrington believes that television and its highly masculine characters are partly responsible for an increase in toxic masculinity.
He continues, “Which is innate and what is learned?
What do you learn on TV and on the streets that make young boys know that they have to be a certain kind to be men?
In my opinion, this is one of the great questions of our time – how can we change this?
“Young men have something really wrong.”
In the interview, he also admits that he would not participate in the production of the Precursors or Sequels of the Throne Game after the series ends next summer, because, according to his own words, he has “had enough of the battlefields and horses.”
In November, Harrington will appear in a new version of Sam Shepard's play True West, which tells the story of the film producer and his robber brother.
The actor recently revealed that getting acquainted with his future wife Rose was the best thing she's ever been offered.
‘I met my wife on this show, so in a way, it gave me my future family and my life since then,’ she says.
Rose Leslie played Ygritte, Harrington’s Jon Snow’s Love in an Emmy Award-winning fantasy series.
The couple were married in June 2018 on the Leslie family's estates in Scotland.
HIV/AIDS: The number of new infections in China has increased by 14%
China has reported a 14% increase in the number of HIV-positive and AIDS-infected citizens.
According to health officials, the virus affects the lives of more than 820 000 people in the country.
Approximately 40 000 new cases have been reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
A clear majority of new cases have been mediated through sex, which represents a change from previous statistics.
Traditionally, HIV has spread rapidly in some parts of China through contaminated blood transfusions.
However, according to Chinese health officials at a conference in Yunnan province, such HIV infections have been reduced to almost zero.
A year later, however, the number of HIV-infected and AIDS sufferers in China has risened by 100 000 cases.
HIV infections through sex are an acute issue in China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is reported to be widespread.
Investigations have estimated that due to the country's conservative values, 70-90% of men who have sex with men eventually get married to women.
Many cases of diseases are caused by insufficient protection in such relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged to guarantee the universal availability of HIV medicines as part of efforts to solve the problem.
Maxine Waters denies the employee’s leaked Republican senator information and accuses of spreading “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories”
Maxine Waters, a U.S. member of the U.S. House of Representatives, denied claims on Saturday that her employee had posted personal information about three Republican senators on Wikipedia pages.
A Democrat from Los Angeles asserted that the allegations were distributed by “extremist right-wing” commentators and websites.
‘Lies, lies and more despicable lies,’ said Waters in a statement on Twitter.
According to this information, South Carolina-representing Senator Lindsey Graham and Senators Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch from the state of Uthat were home addresses and phone numbers.
An unidentified person released the information Thursday from the U.S. Congressory during a hearing on the Senate's commission, including accusations of sexual abusivities against Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh.
The leak occurred shortly after three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that according to Hillin newspaper, the messages were posted from an IP address attached to Waters' office and by a member of Waters staff.
‘This unfounded accusation is completely wrong and it’s an absolute lie.’
“My employee, whose identity, personal data and security have been compromised as a result of these fraudulent and false accusations, are in no way responsible for leaking this information.
This unfounded accusation is completely false and absolutely a lie.”
Waters' statement was quickly criticized on the Internet, and Criticises were accompanied by former White House Press Secretary Ari Fleischer.
‘The way he denies this is very hostile,’ wrote Fleischer.
‘It seems that his character is not suitable for a member of Congress.
When a person is accused of something he hasn't done, there's no reason to behave hostilely.
There's no reason for defiance.
There's no reason to question the prosecutor's motives.
The situation should behave in moderation and calmness.”
Fleischer appeared to compare Waters' response to Democrat's criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who was accused of excessive hostility during Thursday's hearing.
Republican nominee Omar Navarro, who competes for Waters' place in congressional mid-elections, has also expressed his views on Twitter.
‘Unbelievable if this is true,’ he tweeted.
Waters said in a statement that it had contacted “the appropriate authorities and law enforcement bodies as a result of these fraudulent accusations.
‘We will make sure the perpetrators are exposed,’ he continued, “and that they will be legally held accountable for all destructive and dangerous actions directed at any member of my staff.”
Johnny English strikes once again – an under-intensity Rowan Atkinson agent parody
It’s already a well-established tradition to look for Brexit references from any film tilted towards the UK. However, this doesn’t seem to be suitable for the Johnny English agent parody and the action comedy series, which started in 2003 with the Johnny English track and resurrected with the 2011 sequel ‘Johnny English – Reborn’.
Can tongue-in-cheek nation satire become Britain's new national export success?
Either way, the glarious-eyed, cumin-faced and incompetent Johnny English has earned his permission to screw up for the second time. First and foremost, his name speaks to the fact that this is a comic creation aimed at a film market outside the English-speaking world.
Of course, it's about that tolvana secret agent who desperately strives to despise to spirture the glamourous glamoure that's going on, but it's a mixture of Inspector Clouseau, Mr. Bean, and that unhappy one note musician at London's opening concert in 2012.
The character's basis is a cosmopolitan and mystical man of the world, who was originally portrayed by Barclaycard's TV commercials.
The most recent performance has its moments.
I especially liked the scene where Johnny English arrives by helicopter dressed in medieval armor, and the rotor's blades bruise his helmet.
Atkinson’s physical comedy talents come out, but the humour itself seems under-intensified and strangely unnecessary, especially because ‘serious’ film brands such as 007 and Dangerous Mission today offer humor as part of the film’s central content.
Humor seems to be aimed at children, and Johnny English's crazy treatment doesn't seem as inventive and sharp-sighted to me as Atkinson's comedy of Mr. Bean's silent roles.
The premise that is repeated every year is that the United Kingdom is in much trouble.
A cyber hacker has infiltrated Britain's top-secret spy network and reveals the identities of all British agents on the field to the dismay of an agent employed – which is portrayed as Kevin Eldon's regrettably small performance.
This is the final straw for the prominent prime minister, who is already up to his ears in the swamp of political unpopularity: Emma Thompson is doing everything she can in the skins of the Teresa May parody character, but the script just doesn't provide her with enough space.
When the intelligence service's advisers inform him that all the spies in the field have been exposed, it is necessary to invite someone who has already stepped aside from active service.
Of course, it’s a tweaking Johnny English in his own personality. He spends his retirement as a teacher in fancy school, but at the same time gives unofficial lessons in the noble art of espionage. The topic stirs up some clever jokes; English holds School of Rock-type lessons in the secrets of reconnaissance.
English will be rushed to an emergency meeting in Whitehalli, where he will once again meet his endlessly patient fighting partner Bough, still played by Ben Miller.
Bough is already a family man, married to a submarine commissioner whose role Vicki Pepperdine doesn't seem to come in.
Thus, the catastrophic Batman and Robin have returned to His Majesty's secret service to confront Ophelia Bhuletova, a fatal super criminal played by Olga Kurylenko.
In the meantime, the prime minister is being manipulated by a charismatic IT billionaire. Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy, claims to be able to solve British computer problems.
The odyssey of English and Bough immediately turns into a farce: a duo disguised as waitresses sets fire to a fine French restaurant and causes chaos as they secretly seek Volta's luxury yacht. The attempt to familiarize itself with Volta's house with the help of virtual glasses ends up in pure anarchy in the real world.
Even the last inhibitions of the authors disappeare when coming to the final scene, but despite all the powerlessness and charm, the film still feels a bit done for the child audience.
The film can be considered moderate.
Looking at Johnny English films, I've ended up thinking the same thing before: why can't the British film industry give Rowan Atkinson a role that would do justice to his talent?
The British Workers' Party denies plans for a four-day labour week with a five-day salary
Led by Jeremy Corbyn, Labour is pondering a radical plan according to which Britons would work four days a week, but their wages would be paid for five days.
The party is said to require business leaders to transfer the savings generated by the artificial intelligence revolution to the workers in the form of an extra day off.
According to the plan, employees could enjoy three-day weekends with the same salary.
According to sources, the idea would ‘fit’ into the party’s economic agenda and its plans to tilt politics for the benefit of the workers.
The Confederation of Trade Unions supports the transition to a four-day working week as a means by which workers could benefit from the changing economic situation.
‘High-Level Workers' Party source tells the Sunday Times newspaper: ‘The review of the policy should be announced before the end of the year.
The change won’t happen overnight, but a four-day working week is a goal that fits the party’s approach of balancing the economy for the benefit of workers, as well as the party’s overall industrial strategy.”
The Workers' Party is not the first party to support the idea: Britain's Green Party pledged to support the transition to a four-day working week in connection with its 2017 referendum campaign.
However, this objective does not receive indivisible support among Labour.
A spokesman for the Labour Party states: “A four-day working week is not the party’s policy, and the party is not considering switching to one.”
John McDonnell, Treasury Secretary of the Shadow Government, took advantage of last week's labour conference to present his vision for the economic revolution.
McDonnell said he was determined to recover power from “faceless dictators” and “speculators” of non-professional enterprises.
According to Varjohallitus' finance minister's plans, the current shareholders of water companies would not regain their share as a whole, as the Labour-led government could implement “reductions” on the basis of detected irregularities.
He also confirmed the plans to place employees on the boards of directors of companies and to create funds for inclusive ownership, which would transfer 10 per cent of the share capital of private sector companies to the management of employees. According to the plan, an individual employee would be paid a maximum of 500 pounds in dividends each year.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy are discussing in the 60 Minutes program about whether the FBI's Investigation on Kavanaugh could change their minds
Investigation on the allegations against FBI Judge Brett Kavanaugh has delayed at least a week of a final vote on Kavanaugh's appointment as a Supreme Councillor. The delay also raises the question of whether the outcome of the investigation could undermine the support of some Republican senators for Kavanaugh's appointment.
In an episode of The 60 Minutes on Sunday, Scott Pelley asked Republican Senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham if it was possible for the FBI to find out things that would allow them to change their minds.
Kennedy appeared more open compared to his South-Carolinean colleague.
‘Of course,’ Kennedy said.
‘I said when I went to the hearing... I said I’ve had a conversation with Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after this happened when the accusations came up, and I asked him ‘Did you do it?’
He was determined, determined, unequivocal.”
Graham's voice, on the other hand, looks set in stone.
‘I’ve made my decision about Brett Kavanaugh and it’s bomb-proof,’ he said.
‘Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I know the following: Brett vehemently denied it,’ Graham added, citining Christine Blasey Ford.
“None of the people he named were able to confirm.
It's a 36-year-old thing.
I don’t see what could change.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival, and what has it done to reduce poverty?
On Saturday, the Global Citizen Festival in New York, is a musical event whose performer team is as impressive as its mission: eradicating poverty from the world.
For the seventh time, the Global Citizen Festival attracts tens of thousands of people on the lawns of New York's Central Park to listen to performers such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also promote the real goal of the event: eradicating extreme poverty by 2030.
Started in 2012, Global Citizen Festival is a follow-up to the Global Powerty Project, an international advisory group aimed at eradicating poverty by increasing the number of people who are active in working to eradicate poverty.
In order to receive a free ticket to the event (if you are not willing to pay for a VIP ticket), the audience must complete a number of tasks or “deeds”, such as volunteering, sending an e-mail to a political decision-maker or making a phone call, or performing any other action that disseminates awareness of the goals of the project.
But how has Global Citizen succeeded now that it's only 12 years to achieve the goal?
Is there a real way to persuade people to take action with a free concert ticket, or is it just one ‘cliktivism’ among others – the idea that the world can be changed by signing an online petition or submitting a tweet?
Global Citizen has recorded more than 19 million “deeds” in favor of different goals since 2011.
According to it, these actions have encouraged political decision-makers to announce commitments and principles equivalent to more than 37 billion dollars that have been used in a way that will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group listed 390 commitments and declarations, on the basis of which more than 10 billion dollars have already been spent or collected.
The group estimates that the proceeds have already affected the lives of nearly 649 million people worldwide.
Most important commitments include, for example, The Power of Nutrition, a UK-based group of investors and executors who have pledged to “help children develop to their full potential.” The group pledged to deliver US$35 million to Rwanda to end malnutrition in the country. This happened after activists sent them 4,700 tweets as a result of the Global Citizen campaign.
“Thanking the British government, donors, governments from different countries, and citizens like you, we can transfer social injustice and malnutrition to history magazines,” Power of Nutrition envoy Tracey Ullman said at a concert in London in 2018.
According to the group, the British government was also urged to take action to improve the nutrition of mothers and children with more than 5 000 actions. The government has announced funding for the Power on Nutrition project, which focuses on combating malnutrition, which reaches 5 million mothers and children.
One of the questions in the Frequently Asked Questions column on the movement’s website is, ‘why do you think we can put an end to extreme poverty?’
Global citizen’s answer: “The road will be long and difficult, and many stumbling blocks will lie ahead of us.
However, we will succeed, such as major civil rights struggles and anti-apartheid movements before us, because together we are stronger.”
Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman will host a New York concert this year, starring artists such as Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe.
According to the U.S. Department of Interiority, the country could complicate energy exports by using the fleet as an “obstacle”
According to U.S. Secretary of the Interior Ryan Zinke quoted by the Washington Examiner, Washington could “relieve” his navy to prevent the spread of Russian energy on the world market, including the Middle East.
Zinke argues that Russia's activities in Syria – at the invitation of a legitimate government – are an excuse to address new energy markets in the region.
‘I believe that [Russia’s] reason for presence in the Middle East is the transmission of energy, just like in Eastern Europe, in the southern half of Europe,” Zinken said.
According to the office holder, there are ways and means to prevent this.
“The United States has the ability to keep shipping routes open with the help of their fleet and, if necessary, by blockading to ensure that the energy they provide does not end up on the world market,” he said.
Zinke spoke to an event organized by the Energy Consumers' Union to an audience. It's a non-profit group profiled as a “mouthpiece for energy consumers” in the United States.
He went on by comparing Washington's approaches to Russia and Iran by pointing out that it's one and the same thing.
“The economic option between Iran and Russia is more or less speculation, and replacing the fuel with another,” he says, describing Russia as ‘one-stunt circus number’ whose economy is based on fossil fuels.
The statements take place in a situation where the Trump administration has sought to intensify exports of liquefied natural gas to Europe and use it to compensate for the significantly more favorable Russian liquefied natural gas.
In order to intensify this, the Trump administration, including President Trump, are trying to persuade Germany to withdraw from the “inappropriate” Nord Stream 2 plumb project, which, according to President Trump, ties Berlin to Moscow’s leash.
Moscow is repeatedly focused on the Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, which aims to double the transmission capacity of the in-use pipeline 110 billion cubic meters, is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin argues that there are purely economic reasons behind Washington's opposition and sees it as an example of unhealthy competition.
‘I believe that we share the view that energy cannot be an instrument of pressure, and that consumers should have the opportunity to choose its supplier,’ said Russian Energy Minister Alexander Novak after meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
The U.S. attitude has triggered a counter-reaction in Germany, which has reinforced its commitment to the project.
The country's leading umbrella organisation for industry, the Confederation of German Industries, has urged the United States to stay away from the EU's energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
“I find it very problematic that a third country interferes with our energy supply,” Dieter Kempf, head of the Confederation of German Industry, recently said after a meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren, Senator from Massachusetts, is going to think carefully” about nominating in the 2020 presidential election
Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts announced on Saturday that she was “reflecting carefully” to become a presidential nominee after the midterm elections.
Warren said he was considering candidacy at an open-claim quiz in Holyoke, Massachusetts.
‘It’s time for women to move to Washington and repair our defective administration, and this also means a woman in the leadership of the Kingdom,’ she said, according to The Hill.
“After the sixth day of Novemy, I will carefully reflect on whether to run for president.”
Warren judged President Donald Trump during the event by saying “he’s moving the country in the wrong direction.”
‘I’m worried about what Donald Trump is doing to our democracy,’ he says.
Warren has appeared bluntly criticizing Trump and his Supreme Court nominee, Brett Kavanaugh.
In a tweet on Friday, Warren wrote, “Of course we need an FBI investigation before voting.”
However, according to an opinion poll released on Thursday, the majority of Warren's constituents are not in favour of this nomination in 2020.
According to a study conducted by the Department of Political Science at Suffolk University and the Boston Globe, 58% of “probable” voters in Massachusetts are not in favor of nominating a senator.
32% were in favour of becoming a candidate.
Of those who responded to the survey, more were in favour of former Governor Deval Patrick nominating with a share of 38%, while 48% objected to the idea.
Other prominent Democratic nominees that have emerged during the discussions for the 2020 election include former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.
According to the Associated Press news agency, Biden has said he will formalize his candidacy by January.
Sarah Palin refers to her son's war traumas at Donald Trump's election
Sarah Palin's son Track Palin, 26, spent a year in Iraq after enlisting in the Armed Forces in September.
Track Palin was detained on the night of Monday and charged with domestic violence
“From what my own son will have to go through when he returns home, I can very well identify with the other families facing the consequences of the PTSD and the injuries that will be carried by our soldiers returning home,” he told an audience gathered in Tulsa, Oklahoma, for Donald Trump’s election.
Palin called the arrest ‘threaden in the living room’ and said his son, like other war veterans ‘return home a little changed, they will return hardened, they will return wondering whether he and his comrades in the army and air force have done for this land.”
Track Palin was detained on Monday in Wasilla, Alaska. According to Wasilla police spokesman Dan Bennett, he was charged with domestic violence and assault on a woman, as well as domestic violence and handling of a gun while intoxicated.
18 states and the Column District of Columbia are in support of the lawsuit filed against the new Asylum Policy
Eighteen states and the Companities of Columbia are appealing for a new U.S.-enforcement statement that asylum will not be granted to people fleeing gang violence or domestic violence.
Representatives of 18 states and districts have filed a ‘Friends of Justice’ letter on Friday in Washington, D.C., supporting asylum seekers who violate the policy, according to NBC News.
The plaintiff's full name has not been announced in the so-called Grace vs. Sessions lawsuit filed by the American Civil Liberties Union in August.
He says his partner ‘and his sons belonging to the violent gang’ abused him. The U.S. authorities denied him asylum on 20.7.
He was arrested in Texas.
A public prosecutor who supported Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, a large number of U.S. asylum seekers, as countries where gang and domestic violence are a problem that has spread to all levels of society.
The U.S. new asylum policy overturned the Appeals Board of Asylum Applications in 2014, according to which undocumented immigrants have been able to seek asylum on the basis of domestic violence.
Karl Racine, a public prosecutor in the Column District of Columbia, said in a statement on Friday that a new policy would “ignorate state, federal, and international law for decades.”
‘Federal law requires all applications for asylum to be settled on the basis of application-specific facts and circumstances, and such an obstacle violates this principle’ ‘lovers’ says.
Prosecutors also argued in the letter that asylum seekers are blocking the practice of damaging the U.S. economy, as newcomers are likely to become entrepreneurs and “bring the necessary additional workforce.”
In June, General Prosecutor Jeff Sessions ordered judges dealing with immigration issues to suspend the granting of asylum to victims fleeing domestic violence and gang violence.
‘Asylum is intended for persons fleeing their home country due to the persecution or fear caused by racial, religion, nationality or a particular social class, or political opinion.’ Sessions announced a new asylum policy in its 11.6. statement.
Asylum was never intended to solve all the problems – even serious problems – that people face all over the world on a daily basis.
Desperate rescue of the survivors continues in Palu – the number of fatalities found has already doubled
For survivors, the situation becomes even more serious.
‘The situation is very tense,’ says 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma as she calms down her febrile baby at the evacuation centre in Palu’s ruined city.
“Ambulances bring new bodies a minute by minute.
“Clean water is scarce.”
Residents return to their devastated homes, pick up their weathed things and try to save what can still be saved.
Hundreds of people have been injured and hospitals damaged by the 7.5 magnitude quake are full.
Some of the injured, such as Dwi Haris, who suffers from spinal and shoulder fractures, rest outside Palu's army hospital, where patients are treated due to constant, intense aftershocks.
His eyes are filled with tears as he recounts how a fierce earthquake shook the fifth-floor hotel room he shared with his wife and daughter.
“We couldn’t save ourselves.
I was squeezed between the ruins of the wall, I think.” Harris says, according to the Associated Press, and tells that he and his family were wedding guests in the city.
‘I heard my wife scream for help and then it was quiet.
I don't know what's happened to him or my child.
“I hope they’ll be safe.”
U.S. ambassador accuses China of ‘provoking’ with ‘propaganda announcements’
The U.S. ambassador to China accuses Beijing of using an American newspaper as a means of propaganda. This happens a week after the Chinese newspaper published a four-page advertisement in a U.S. daily newspaper praising the mutual interests of the U.S.-China trade agreement.
U.S. President Donald Trump on Wednesday referred to the China Daity's paid appendix to the Des Moines Register, the largest newspaper in the state of Iowa, after accusing China of interfering in the 6.11. congression elections. China has denied the claims.
Trump's accusations about Beijing's attempts to interfere in the U.S. election means, according to U.S. officials who have spoken to Reuters, a new stage in Washington's expanding campaign to put pressure on China.
While it's normal for a foreign government to distribute declarations to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently stranded in an ever-escalating trade war, in which both parties take turns imposing customs tariffs on counterparty's export products.
According to Chinese and American experts, at the beginning of the Trade War, China's revenge tariffs were targeted at states such as Iowa, which supported Trump's Republican Party.
Terry Branstad, U.S. Ambassador to China and long-time governor of Iowa State, said Beijing had done damage to U.S. workers, farmers and businesses. Iowa is a large exporter of agricultural products to China.
“China “is now accelerating its provocations even more by publishing propaganda advertisements in our own free press,” Branstad wrote in an opinion piece published in Sunday’s Des Moines Register.
‘By spreading its propaganda, the Chinese government will take advantage of the U.S.’s esteemed free speech and press traditions by placing a paid announcement in the Des Moines Register,’ Branstad writes.
“On the other hand, there are only a limited number of differing opinions in newspaper kiosks on the streets of Beijing, and real, dissenting opinions that Chinese citizens may have about the alarming development of their finances cannot be seen at all. This is due to the fact that the media is firmly on the leash of the Chinese Communist Party,” he writes.
Branstad goes on to say that “one of China’s biggest newspapers dodged an offer to publish” an article he wrote, but doesn’t specify which magazine it’s about.
Kavanaugh fiasco expels female voters from Republicans, analysts warn
The fact that many Republican top names stand behind the Supreme Countient Judicial Candidate Brett Kavanaugh and defend him may, according to analysts, counter-reaction especially among female voters in the upcoming mid-election.
The emotions surrounding the incident have taken a big tour, and most Republicans have already announced that they want to proceed with the vote.
‘These things can’t be reversed,’ said Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Syracuse University’s Maxwell Institute, in an article published Saturday.
Reeher doesn't believe Senator Jeff Flaken (Republicans, Arizona) last-minute bid for FBI Investigation will appease angry voters.
‘Women won’t forget what happened yesterday and they won’t forget tomorrow or in November,’ says Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and spokesperson for the National Reformation group MoveOn, according to a newspaper in the capital on Friday.
On Friday morning, protesters sang “November is coming” as they demonstrated in the Senate’s corridors as the Republicans of the Judicial Committee decided to proceed with Kavanaugh’s appointment, which occurred despite Dr. Christine Blasey Ford’s testimony, Mic reported.
“Democratic enthusiasm and motivation will no longer be available for evaluation,” says Stu Rothenberg, an independent political analyst in an interview on the news site.
“People say that enthusiasm has already been high, and that’s true.
But it could be even greater, especially among insecure female voters in the suburbs and younger voters, 18-29-year-olds who don’t often vote because they don’t like the president.”
Analysts are anticipating a possible counter-reaction if Republicans move on to reinforcement even before Ford's certificate speech against the Supreme Court Candidate, in which he made his claim of sexual harassment.
“It’s going to be a mess for the Republican Party,” said Michael Steele, former spokesman of the Republican National Committee’s NBC News at the beginning of last week.
“It’s not just about the committee’s vote or the final vote, it’s about whether Kavanaugh will be questioned. It’s a question of how Republicans have acted on the issue and how they’ve treated Ford,” Guy Cecil, the leader of Priorities USA, who supports the Democrats, remarked.
However, the Americans seem somewhat divided on whether they should believe Ford's or Kavanaugh's testimony when a narrow majority bent to the latter.
According to a new poll conducted by YouGov, 41% of the respondents believe Ford's testimony definitely or most likely, while 35% say they believe Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38% say they think Kavanaugh was likely or unconditionally lying in his witness statement, while only 30% said the same from Ford.
After Flake pushed the matter forward, the FBI is investigating the accusations filed by Ford and at least one other Deborah Ramirez.
In front of the Judiciary Committee, Ford testified under sworn, Kavanaugh harassed him drunk when Ford was 17 years old.
Ramirez claims the Supreme Court's candidate revealed his genitals at a student party while they were studying at Yale University in the 1980's.
The inventor of the World Wide Web is planning to launch a new Internet in response to Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, has launched a start-up company aimed at competiting with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The technology legend's latest project, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee's open-source Solid platform.
Solid allows users to decide where their data is stored, and who has access to which data.
In an interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jokes that Inrupt’s intention is to “dominate the world“.
‘It has to be done now,’ he said, referring to his start-up.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid’s technology so that people can create their own “personal repository” (POD).
It can include contact lists, checklists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify all available in a single browser at the same time.
What's unique in a personal repository is that it's entirely up to the user who has access to what types of data.
The company calls it “personal empowerment through data.”
According to the company's CEO, John Bruce, Inrupt's idea is to provide resources, processes, and the necessary skills to make Solid available to everyone.
The company currently comprises Berners-Lee, a security platform purchased by Bruce, IBM's security platform, some paid developers working with the project, as well as a community of volunteer coders.
Technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized applications with the tools available on Inrupt's website. All of this will be available starting this week.
Berners-Lee says he and his team won’t discuss “with Facebook and Google about whether people should be offered a complete change where all their business models are turned on their heads overnight.”
“We don’t ask their consent.”
In an article published on Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt’s “task is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem for the unity and quality of the new network built for Solid“.
In 1994, Berners-Lee revolutionized the Internet by founding the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In the last few months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in a debate on net neutrality.
Berners-Lee retains his position as the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute even after Inrupt was launched.
‘I’m extremely optimistic about the next era of the network,’ Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: the city remembers World War First World War's hero priest
The city celebrates the Church of England's only Victoria Cross medal-achieved priest, who served as a soldier, 100 years ago.
The Medal of Braveliness was awarded to Lieutenant Colonel, Pastor Bernard Vann on 29.9.1918 due to the events of the attack in the Bellenglisen and Lehaucourt villages.
However, Vann died four days later without ever learning of the highest British military tribute.
Two of his grandchildren were revealing a memorial stone at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire, last Saturday.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Vann, says the event is “impressively symbolic”; the monument was unveiled exactly 100 years after his award-winning grandfather’s feat.
According to London Gazette, 29 9.1918 Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion over the Canal de Saint-Quent “in a very dense fog and under the gunfire of heavy field guns and machine guns.“
Later, he plunged to the foreline and “pointing at the greatest bravery” led the troops to the field cannon alone. In the ensuing battle, Vann defeated three soldiers of the cannon radiator with his own hands.
Colonel Vann died the creation of a German sniper on 4.10.1918 – just one month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather’s act was “something I could never achieve, something that inspires respect.”
Michael Van He and his brother Dr James Vann laid a wreath after a parade with the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann says it’s “great honor to take part in the parade” and added: “A great number of people are remembering the glory of a true hero.”
MMA lovers waited all night for Bellator 206 tournament – the TV channel showed Pipsa Pig
Imagine a situation where you've been staying up all night to watch the anticipated Bellator 206 tournament, and there won't be an entire event.
There were 13 fights, including six major league fights, and the show was scheduled to be broadcast live on Channel 5 in the UK.
At six o'clock in the morning, just as Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were getting ready for the ring, the show switched to Pipsa Pig to the great dismay of the viewers.
Some of the spectators didn't really feel happy after staying up all night due to the match.
One MMA lover opened up on Twitter, describing replacing the fight with a children’s cartoon as “some sort of sick joke.”
‘According to the law, this content was not appropriate to be presented at six o’clock in the morning, so they had to switch it to a children’s program,’ explained Dave Schwartz, Bellator’s senior vice president of marketing and communication, changing the program.”
‘Peppa Piggy, yes.’
Scott Coker, the head of Bellator, said schedules would be being worked to allow UK spectators to watch what's going to happen in the future.
‘I’m thinking of a retransmission that will probably make the program work,’ Coker said.
“However, it’s six o’clock in the morning there, and we can’t get the program scheduled until Sunday, which in turn is Monday their time.
We're working on it.
You can believe that when the program changed in their heads, messages began to travel in one direction and the other, and they were not all friendly.
We tried to fix the error because we thought it was a technical problem.
However, this was not the case, it was a legislative matter.
I can promise that next time this will not happen.
We're limiting the number of matches to five instead of six, as usual. We tried to offer our fans more and tried too much.
That’s an unfortunate situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley Felt Inferior to His Sexuality
Diving jumper and Olympic athlete Tom Daley says he felt inferior to everyone else because of his sexuality, but this motivates him for success.
The 24-year-old athlete says he didn’t notice until he came to middle school that “not everyone is like me.”
Daley, sent by Lauren Laverne on Desert Island Discs on Radio 4, says she spoke about gay rights in order to give others ‘hope’.
He also explains how it wasn't so important to win the Olympics after having a child.
Kristy Young, the regular presenter of a long-time radio show, has taken several months off due to his illness.
While appearing as a guest on Laverne’s first show, Daley said she felt ‘less’ when she was young than everyone else, because ‘it wasn’t socially acceptable to like boys and jobs.’
He says: “Until today, these emotions, to feel less and different have been reality, and they have given me the strength and energy I have needed to succeed.”
He says he wanted to show that he was ‘something’ so that he doesn’t disappoint everyone when his sexuality was finally revealed.
The two-time Olympic bronze medalist has become a high-profile LGBT activist, appearing at this year's Commonwealth Games appealing to the criminalization of homosexuality in other countries.
He said he came forward because he feels lucky to be able to live openly without consequences, and wanted to ‘hope’ for other people.
The three-time world champion talks about falling in fall in love with a man – American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013 – “as a surprise.”
Daley married this 20-year-older Oscar winner last year, but says the age difference has never been a problem.
‘If you experience so many things when you’re so young’ – he took part in his first Olympics at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later – ‘it’s hard to find another person of the same age who would’ve experienced the same kind of prospiencies and adversity,’ Daley says.
The couple had a son in June – Robert Ray Black-Daley – as a result of which Daley’s “whole perspective” has changed.
“If you’d asked me last year, my life would’ve revolved around winning a gold medal,” he says.
“You know, there are bigger things than a golden Olympic medal.
Robbie is my Olympic gold medal.”
His son was named after Daley's father, who died of brain cancer at the age of just 40 in 2011.
Daley said his father would never accept that he would die, and one of the most recent things he asked Daley about tickets to the 2012 Olympics. He wanted to sit in the front row.
“I couldn’t tell him he wasn’t going to be there anymore to be in the front row,” Daley said.
‘I held him’s hand when his breath stopped. It wasn’t until when his breath had really stopped and he was dead that I finally realized that he wasn’t invulnerable,’ he says.
The following year, Daley competed in the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
‘I knew this is something I’ve been dreaming of my whole life—jumping in front of a home crowd at the Olympics. There’s no better feeling,’ Daley says.
This was also an inspiration for his first choice of songs in the broadcast – Heather Small’s song “Proud” – which evoked emotions already as he was preparing for the Olympics, and which still gets his skin goosebumps.
Desert Island Discs will be broadcast on BBC Radio 4 on Sundays at 11:15 BST.
Mickelson's half-fit bench at Saturday's Ryder Cup
Phil Mickelson of the United States breaks the record on Sunday by playing his 47th Ryder Cup, but he has to make a lot of effort to avoid a record-breaking record-breaking record in history as an unhappy performance.
Mickelson, who will be playing for the 12th time in a two-year event for the 12th time, was taken to the bench by Captain Jim Furyk for the duration of fourball and foursome on Saturday.
In general, the five-time-threshong Major winner, who is the center of attention, now spent half the day as a cheerleader and the other half on the pitch trying to get hold of his shot.
Mickelson, a 48-year-old who wasn't even at the top of his career as one of the most accurate drivers, isn't an ideal choice for the tight Le Golf National course, where the long-growing roughman punishes inaccurate shots.
If there isn't enough opposition to Mickelson himself, Sunday's ninth game will be played by British Open winner Francesco Molinari, who is playing with rookie Tommy Fleetwood, with the goal of winning all four wins this week.
If the Americans, who are four points behind when they come in 12 singles, get a flying start, Michelson's game could prove extremely important.
Furyk has expressed his confidence in his player, but whatever else you could say in the situation.
“He perfectly understands the role he played today. He clapped me on the back, squinted, and said he’s ready for tomorrow,” Furyk said.
“He has a lot of self-confidence.
He's undoubtedly one of the best players and he's brought so much to his team in the past, and this week.
I probably didn't think he'd play two games.
I imagined otherwise, but that's how it went and we came up with such a solution.
“He wants to be on that field as much as anyone else.”
Mickelson breaks Nick Faldo's record for several Ryder Cup matches on Sunday.
It can also mark the end of a man's Ryder Cup career, which, admittedly, was never comparable to his personal performance.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 losses and seven halves, but according to Furyk, his presence brings something unspecified to the team.
‘He’s funny, he’s sarcastic, he’s clever, he likes joking at people’s expense, and he’s a funny guy that everyone likes,’ he explains.
“Younger players must have had a lot of fun with him this week, and that’s amusing to watch.
He brings so much more into the team than just his contribution in the game.”
The European team's captain, Thomas Bjorn, knows how long the lead can quickly disappear
Thomas Bjorn, the captain of the European team, knows from experience that there can be a bumped-up journey from the Ryder Cup's large-scaled lead before the last-day duel.
The Danish player made his debut in Valderrama in 1997, with Seve Ballesteros had a five-point lead over the Americans, but who narrowly emerged as the winner on the lime lines by the length of his nose and won with the narrowest possible margin141⁄2–131⁄2.
“It’s a reminder of how we lead briskly in Valderrama, we led briskly in Brookline, where we lost, but we won in Valderrama, albeit narrowly,” said Bjorn, after watchinging the 2018 team’s 5–3 victories both on Friday and yesterday, moving to a 10–6 lead on the Le Golf National course.
History will show both me and all the other members of the team that this is not over yet.
Tomorrow we'll shoot with a big caliber.
We'll get to the field and do everything right.
The game won't be over until the numbers are on the board.
We have a goal, we're trying to win that prize, and that's all we're aiming for.
As I’ve always said, I’m going to focus on these 12 players who play on our side, but we’re aware of what’s on the other side: the best players in the world.”
Delighted at how his team’s players have performed on a difficult field, Bjorn adds: “I’ll never lick until I fall for things like this.
Tomorrow's a completely different thing.
Tomorrow, we'll give priority to individual performances, and that's when we'll talk about different things.
It's great to be on the pitch with a partner when everything's going well, but when you're on the field alone, then the player's capacity will be tested to the bottom.
This is a message that must be conveyed to the players – tomorrow everyone will be able to achieve the best performance
Now we have to give up the partner, who is also allowed to go [to the field alone] and do his best.”
Contrary to Bjorn's, opposing captain Jim Furyk will demand a better performance from his players as individuals than they were able to do as partners, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who scored three points out of four.
Furyk, on the other hand, has experienced full turns of the last day in both directions. He was part of Brookline’s winning team before ending up as a loser when the European team defeated “Medinah Miraculously”.
‘I remember every damn word,’ he answered the question of how the 1999 team captain Ben Crenshaw handed out his team’s ranks for the last day.
“We’ll have 12 important games tomorrow, but we’ll want to get a quick start just as it happened at Brookline, or as it happened in Medinah.
When you get it moving in one direction, it puts a lot of pressure on the middle games.
We’ve adjusted our line-ups accordingly and we’re going to put the boys on the pitch the way we want, as you know, we’re going to work miracles tomorrow.”
Thomas has been tasked to try to lead the defensive battle and will face Rory McIlroy in the most important game. Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter are the next to face their European opponents.
‘I put the team’s friends in this order, because I think it’ll carry through the entire tournament,’ Bjorn commented.
Germany's new warship delayed again
The newest frigate of the German Navy should have been deployed in 2014. The vessel intends to replace obsolete Cold-War equipment, but it will be commissioned at the earliest next year due to defective systems and exponentially increasing costs, according to local media.
According to a military spokesperson who spoke to Die Zeit, ‘Rheinland-Palatinate’, the deployment of the first vessel of the new Baden-Württemberg frigate class has been postponed until the first half of 2019.
The vessel was scheduled to join the fleet in 2014, but an ambitious project has been persecuted by worrying backsliding problems.
The four Baden-Württemberg-categrified vessels commissioned by the Navy in 2007 are intended to replace obsolete Bremen-class frigates.
According to the information available, they will be equipped with high-efficiency cannons, missile systems to combat aerial and surface vessels and stealth technology, such as radar reflectivity, infrared and acoustic signal detectability technology.
The vessel's important features also include a longer maintenance interval – new frigates should be able to operate at sea for up to two years without visiting the home port.
However, continuous delays mean that warships, which represent the pinnacle of technology – whose purpose is said to be to improve Germany’s influence in overseas territories – are already obsolete when they enter the service, the Die Zeit newspaper points out.
The unlucking F125 frigate made headlines last year when the German Navy formally declined to introduce the vessel and returned it to Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This is the first time that the fleet has returned the vessel after its delivery.
Little is known about the reasons for the return, but there has been talk in the German media about the essential “software and hardware failures” that make the warship unsuitable for combat missions.
Software deficiencies are particularly pivotal due to the fact that the Baden-Württemberg-class crew is approximately 120, which is only half the crews of older Bremen-class frigates.
In addition, it turned out that the vessel is remarkably overweight, reducing its performance and limiting the Navy's chances of subsequent upgrades.
Rheinland-Pfalz, with a capacity of 7 000 tonnes, is believed to be twice as heavier than Germany's Second World War.
In addition to defective equipment, the price tag of the entire project is also becoming a problem.
The price is said to have risen to a stunning sum of 3.1 billion euros, while the original price was 2.2 billion euros.
Problems with the introduction of the new frigates have surfaced when German shipping has recently been warned to shrink.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the German Parliament’s Defense Committee, pointed out that the Navy’s usable vessels are “running away.”
According to the authorities, the problem has increased over time due to the decommission of old vessels, but no new ones have been replaced.
He complained that it has not been possible to introduce any Baden-Württemberg-class frigates to the Navy.
An environmental group eavesdropes the hidden life of bats
A new study will be carried out in an area in the highlands of Scotland to find out how bats take advantage of the terrain while hunting for food.
It is hoped that the new study will bring new light to the behavior of these unique flying mammals and provide guidance in future conservation measures.
A study carried out by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland monitors bummed and dwarf bats, as well as earworms and wingers in the Inverewe Garden of the Botanical Garden in Wester Ross.
In the study, special storage devices are placed at important points in the area to monitor the operation of bats during the season.
Employees and volunteers also perform mobile measurements using portable observation devices.
Frequencies of the vows, as well as the species in question, are ensured by the analysis of experts.
Based on the results, a habitat map and report are created, in which bat behaviour can be presented in an image that follows the scale of the area.
Rob Dewar, the organization's nature conservation adviser, hopes the results reveal which habitat areas are the most important for bats, and how different species take advantage of them.
The information is helpful in the planning of habitat areas, such as creating meadows and managing forest areas in such a way that bats and related species are taken into account.
Bat populations in Scotland and the UK as a whole have declined considerably over the last hundred years.
Construction and earthworks threaten the resting places and habitats of bats.
Wind turbines and illumination can also pose a risk, as well as fly paper and certain chemicals used in building materials, as well as attacks on pet cats.
Bats aren't actually blind.
However, due to nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful in catching prey than eyes.
Bats use advanced echoking technology to detect insects and obstacles on their trajectory.
The National Trust for Scotland is an organization dedicated to the protection of the rural environment, which maintains more than 270 historic buildings, 38 significant gardens and 76 000 hectares across the country – and they take bats seriously.
It employs ten specialists who regularly carry out questionnaires, inspections of resting places and, in some cases, animal rescue operations.
The organization has even established Scotland's first and only bat-protected conservation area, Dumfries, and Threave Manor in the Galloway region. Eight of Scotland's ten bat species are found in the area.
According to manager David Thompson, the area is ideal for bats.
‘Threave is very suitable for bats,’ he says.
“We have old buildings and trees and overall a good living environment.
We don’t know everything about bats yet, so our work here and in other areas of the organization will help us better understand what bats need to succeed.”
He emphasizes how important it is to inspect buildings for bats before going to refurbish, as disposing of one bat's resting place can inadvertently cause the deaths of up to 400 female bats and chicks and eradicate an entire local population.
Bats are protected animals and it is forbidden to kill, harass or destroy their resting places.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish official of the Bat Conservation Organization, encourages the public to participate in conservation work.
He says, “We still have a lot to learn from our bats, and we don’t yet know how their populations will survive.”
Ronaldo denies rape allegations while lawyers prepare lawsuit against German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo has labeled the accusations against him as “fake news” and says that people want to “promote themselves” using his name.
Ronaldo's lawyers have taken action to sue the German magazine Der Spiegel.
The Portuguese and Juventus striker has been charged with the rape of an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga in a Las Vegas hotel in 2009.
He allegedly paid the victim US$370,000 in secrecy, Der Spiegel reported on Friday.
Ronaldo appeared on an Instagram stream to 142 million followers just hours after making the allegations. The 33-year-old footballer sharply rejected the accusations and described them as “fake news.”
“No, no, no, no.
“What have they been saying today, fake news,” the five-time Ballon d’Or winner told the camera.
“They just want to promote themselves using my name.
It's normal. It's normal.
They want to be famous by using my name, it's part of this profession.
“I’m a happy man and everything’s fine,” the player continued with a smile.
Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel for allegations they’ve called “unworthiness reporting of suspicions of privacy.”
Christian Schertz, a lawyer, said the player demanded compensation for “spiritual affections according to the seriousness of the violation, which probably means the most serious violation of personal rights in recent years.”
The alleged incident is said to have taken place in June 2009 at the Palms Hotel and Casino's suite in Las Vegas.
Ronaldo and Mayorgan are said to have met at a nightclub and moved into the player's room, where he allegedly raped him anally, according to papers handed out to the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga claims Ronaldo fell to his kneets after the alleged incident and said he “was 99% a good guy,” but “one percent” was disappointing.
According to the documents, Ronaldo has confirmed that the couple had had sex, but it was a matter of mutual consent.
Mayorga also claims to have visited a police station and took photographs of her injuries in the hospital, but later ended up with a settlement because she was “horrified by the possibility of vengeancy” and worried that she would “be subjected to public humiliation.”
The 34-year-old says he is now seeking annulment of the settlement because he is still traumatized by the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was close to joining Real Madrid from Manchester United at the time of the alleged assault, and moved to the Italian power-facing team Juventus with a 100-million-dollar contract.
Brexit: Britain ‘would ‘repent forever’ car production
“The UK would “regress forever” if it lost its position as the world’s leading automaker after Brexit, Economy Secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that he finds “worrying” Toyota’s UK unit’s announcement to temporarily suspend production in Burnaston at a factory near Derby if the UK leaves the EU without a deal.
‘We need a deal,’ Clark said.
The Japanese car manufacturer says the impact of border delays will be reflected in job loss if Brexit is reached without a contract.
The factory in Burnaston – producing Toyota's Auris and Avensis models – produced almost 150 000 cars last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union.
“My view is that if the UK suddenly leaves the EU at the end of March, there will be interruptions in the production of our factory,” says Marvin Cooke, CEO of Toyota’s Burnaston unit.
Other car manufacturers operating in Britain, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have expressed fears about exiting the European Union in terms of how cross-border trade will work.
BMW, for example, has announced that it will close its Mini-manufacturing factory for a month when the Brexit is completed.
According to car manufacturers, the most important concern relates to supply chain-related risks in the event of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's production line is runned on a timely JOT basis, where parts arrive at the factory every 37 minutes from their suppliers from the UK and the EU, and the cars are manufactured on request.
If the UK leaves the EU without an agreement on 29 March, it could cause disturbances at the border, which according to the industry could lead to delays and shortages of parts.
It is impossible, according to Toyota, to keep a warehouse that lasts more than a day at the Derbyshire plant, and production would be suspended.
Clark says Theresa May’s plan for EU relations is “carefully calibrated to avoid these checks at the border.”
“We need an agreement. We want the best possible agreement, which not only means success in the current situation, but it’s an opportunity that we have to take advantage of,” he said in a statement.
“Not only Toyota’s statements, but also the statements of other manufacturers, show that we absolutely need to be able to continue the supply chain series, which have worked very well until now.”
Toyota can't tell you how long production is going to be stopped, but warns that additional costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and eventually cause job losses.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has worked in Burnaston for 24 years and serves as a trade union official at the factory, says the concern is growing among members: “According to my experience, lost jobs will never return.
Government spokesman said: “We need to present an accurate and believable plan for our future relations with the EU.”
“Trump’s meeting with Rosenstein may be delayed again,” the White House said
Donald Trump’s challenging meeting with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could “be postponed another week” due to the controversy surrounding Supreme Court Nominee Brett Kavanaugh, the White House announced on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Special Attorney Robert Mueller, which is investigating Russian interference in the elections, the connections of Trump's aides to Russia, and the president's possible efforts to prevent justice.
It has been rumoured in Washington for months that Trump intends to expect the Deputy Attorney General, which would jeopardize the neutrality of Mueller's investigation.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein was discussing the possibility of secretly recording conversations with Trump, as well as the possibility of dismissing the president on the basis of the 25th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution.
Rosenstein has denied the report details.
Rosenstein visited the White House last Monday, and the purpose of the meeting is said to have been Rosenstein's resignation.
At the time, however, President Trump was on a visit to the United Nations in New York, so the day of the meeting was announced Thursday.
Trump said he “doesn’t prefer” to fire Rosenstein, but the meeting was postponed because its timing coincided with a hearing on the Senate Judiciary Committee, in which Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him, Dr Christine Blasey Ford, were both witnesses.
On Friday, Trump ordered an FBI's week-long investigation into Kavanaugh's allegations, which further postponed the Senate's plenary vote.
Trump's press officer Sarah Sanders appeared on Fox News' Sunday show.
When asked about Rosenstein’s meeting, Sanders said: “There’s no date for this. It could be next week, this week, it could be postponed another week due to everything else that’s going on the Supreme Court right now.
It remains to be seen, but I always want to keep the press up to date.”
Some reporters might disagree with this statement: Sanders hasn't held a White House press conference since September 10.
News anchor Chris Wallace asked the reason for this.
Sanders says the reason for the lack of press conferences is due to reasons other than disgusting TV reporters’ ‘observation’, but said: ‘I don’t deny the fact that they’re attention-seeking.’
Sanders, however, implied that direct contacts between Trump and the press would increase.
‘The president holds more questioning hours than any president before him,’ he says, and adds further evidence without quoting: ‘We’ve checked those numbers.’
��‘Press conferences are still going to take place,’ Sanders said, ‘it’s infinitely better if the media have the opportunity to ask questions directly to the President of the United States than to speak with me.
We often try to act in this way and you’ve seen us doing this several times over the past few weeks, and that’s going to replace the press release so you’ll be able to speak directly with the President of the United States.”
Trump regularly responds to questions when leaving the White House or taking part in open events or press conferences with guest dignitaries.
It's rare to perform alone at a press conference.
This week in New York, the president showed possibly the reason for this when performing uninhibitedly and from time to time quirky to the journalists gathered there.
The health minister wrote a letter to employees of the Swedish public health (NHS Scotland) from EU countries regarding fears about Brexit
The Minister of Health has written a letter to the workers of the Scottish health system from EU countries to express their gratitude and hope of staying in the country after Brexit.
Member of the Scottish Parliament, Jeane Freeman, sent a letter with less than six months to leave the United Kingdom.
The Scottish Government is already prepared to cover the costs that will arise from applications from ‘well-established status’ of EU citizens working with specialised public services.
In its letter, Freeman writes: During the summer, negotiations between the United Kingdom and the EU have continued towards the expected decisions in the autumn.
The UK government has also intensified preparations for a possible no-deal resignation.
I know that for all of you, these are uncertain times.
That's why I would like to reiterate now how much we value each of our employees' contributions, regardless of their nationality.
Colleagues from all over and outside the EU bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of healthcare and benefit the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is definitely your home and we really want you to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie for emergency surgery due to head injury
Tennessee State Tigers linebacker Christion Abercrombie has underwent emergency surgery after losing between 31 and 27 to Vanderbilt Commodors on Saturday.
Tennessee State head coach Rod Reed told reporters the injury occurred just before half-time.
“He came to the sideline and simply collapsed into it,” Reed says.
Coaches and medical staff gave Abercrombie oxygen on the sideline before placing him on a stretcher and putting him aside for a more detailed evaluation.
Tennessee State's official told WSMV reporter Chris Harris in Tennessee that Abercrombie had been discharged at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that ‘there is no information about the type/extent of the injury at this point’, and that Tennessee State is trying to figure out when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie is a second-year student at Tennessee State University and is playing for his first season with the team from Illinois after his transfer.
He had five tackles on Saturday before leaving the game, making a total of 18 tackles this season.
Foreign buyers will be subject to a higher stigma tax when they buy real estate in the UK
There will be a higher stigma tax from foreign buyers when they buy real estate in the UK. The extra money will be used according to the Conservative Party's new plans to help the homeless.
The purpose of the change is to neutralize Corby's success in attractinging young voters
Raising stamp tax applies to those who do not pass taxes to the United Kingdom
The Ministry of Finance expects to raise up to 120 million pounds a year – to help homeless people
Theresa May will today announce a higher stamp tax rate from foreign buyers when they build properties in the UK. The extra money will be used to help homeless people.
As an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corby's aspirations to be attracted to young voters. Corby has made promises of more affordable apartments and drew attention to the taxation of high-income earners.
Raising the stamp tax will target people and companies that do not pay taxes to the United Kingdom. The extra money from this will be directed to the government's efforts to ease the plight of the homeless.
The surcharge – which will be added to the current stamp tax two years ago in addition to the second housing and investment housing increases – can be as much as three per cent.
The Minister of Finance expects the increase to generate as many as 120 million pounds annually.
It is estimated that 13 per cent of London's new buildings will be purchased by people who do not live permanently in the UK, which will raise prices and make it more difficult for first-timer buyers to market for owner-occupied dwellings.
Many wealthy areas in the UK – especially in its capital – have become ‘ghost cities’ because several foreign property owners spend most of their time abroad.
The new policy comes just weeks after Boris Johnson proposed lowering the stigma tax as a way to make it easier to buy young people's first homes.
He accused the big construction companies of keeping real estate prices high by buying the land and failing to build it, and urged Prime Minister May to abandon affordable housing quotas to fix Britain’s ‘disgustined housing situation’.
Corbyn has published an eye-catching series of proposals to reform the housing market, including restrictions on rents and the cessation of “negligent evictions.”
He would also like to give councils more power in matters related to the construction of new apartments.
Prime Minister May said: ‘Last year I said I was going to devote the British dream of my prime ministership—that the life of every generation should be better than its predecessor.
This means repairing our broken housing markets.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build their lives here.
However, it cannot be right that people who do not live in the UK, as well as companies located abroad, can buy housing as easily as diligent British residents.
For too many people, owning a home has become far too distant a dream, and the shame of homelessness remains a fact.”
Jack Ross: “My ultimate goal is to run the Scottish national team”
Sunderland manager Jack Ross says his ‘ultimate goal’ is to become manager of the Scottish team at some point.
The 42-year-old Scot is thrilled with the challenge of lifting the nation's north-eastern corner back to their feet. The team is currently in third place in the UK's second division.
Ross moved to the “stadium of lights” this summer after after directing St Mirren back to the Scottish Premier League last season.
“As a player, I wanted to play for my country’s team.
“I got on the second team and that’s it,” Ross told BBC: Scotland’s Sportsound.
“As a child, I often watched Scottish games with my father on Hampden, and it’s something that’s always drawn me back.
Such an opportunity will only come up if I succeed as the club's manager."
Rossi's predecessors as Sunderland's manager include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic's leader says she wasn't worried about stepping into the boots of her well-knowned predecessors in a well-known club. She's previously rejected offers made by Barnsley and Iptswich Town.
“Currently, I’m measuring my own success on whether I can return this club to the Premier League.
Based on the club’s structure and ability, it is undoubtedly part of the Premier League,” he says.
“It’s not easy to get there, but I’d probably only consider myself successful if I can lead the team back to the Premier League.”
Ross has only served as manager for three years: first as Dumbarton's assistant manager, and then for 15 months in the Heats' coaching team.
He then helped Alloa emerge from the relegation games of the third series and elevated St Mirren from relegation fitness to champions over the next season.
Ross says he feels better now than ever in his playing career with Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
‘I was probably at the actual crossroadshield with my career,’ he recalls of the time when he moved into the leadership of Alloa.
“I genuinely thought I’d fit better as a manager than a player.
It sounds strange because I played well, made quite a lot of money and got to enjoy some relatively great successes.
However, gambling can be tough.
Players have to deal with a variety of things every week.
I'm still going through the same things, such as the stress and the pressure of the work, but leading the team feels like just the right thing to do.
I always wanted to lead the team, and now I’m doing it for a living. I feel better in my own body than ever in my adult life.”
The full interview will be listened to in the Sportsound broadcast on Sunday 30.9, Radio Scotdan frequency at 12:00 and 13:00 BST
The perfect time to enjoy a pint is on Saturday at 17.30, the study shows
The heatwave of the summer has given a boost to pubs struggling with Britain's difficulties, but at the same time put more pressure on restaurant chains.
According to the statistics, sales of pub and bar chains grew by 2.7% during July, but restaurant revenues fell by 4.8%.
Peter Martin from CGA said: “Continuing sunny weather and England’s longer-than-expected participation in the FIFA World Cup meaning that July became June-like when pub sales were 2.8% higher, while restaurants had an even worse month.
The 1.8% decline in restaurant sales in June only got worse in July.
Pubs and bars focused on the drinking side were most successful. Their like-for-like sales grew more than the decline in restaurant sales.
Pubs focused on the food side also suffer from heat, but less dramatically than restaurant operators.
It seems that people only wanted to go out drinking in their minds.
Sales of beverages of pubs and bars run by companies increased by 6.6% over the course of the month, and there was a 3% decline in food sales.”
Paul Newman of RSM, a company providing leisure and hotel-restaurant consulting services, says: “The results continue the trend, which has been going on since the end of April.
Weather and major social or sporting events are still the biggest factors influencing the sales of the market for out-of-home meals.
It's no surprise that the struggle between restaurant chains continues, albeit with a 4.8% annual decline in sales, especially now that the industry is currently struggling with cost-pressures.
A long hot summer couldn't have come into a worse seam for operators focused on food, and only time will tell if the milder temperatures in August will bring the respite that the industry desperately needs.
The total overall sales growth of pubs and restaurants, including new opened restaurants, was 2.7% in July, reflecting a slowdown in the creation of new brands.
Coffer Peach Tracker is a barometer that monitors sales in the UK's pub, bar and restaurant industry, which compiles and analyzes profit data from a group operating in 47 sectors with a combined turnover of more than 9 billion pounds. It is considered a reliable measure in the industry.
