An advertisement equating mothers with cows divides opinions online
An advertisement equating mothers to dairy cows by a company that manufactures breast pumps has divided opinions on the Internet.
Elvie, a manufacturer of consumer technology, has launched a product called “the world’s first silent and wearable breast pump”. Its marketing will be accelerated by an advertisement made on the cheek and in the style of music videos, which describes the freedom of a new pump for breastfeeding mothers.
Four real-life mothers dance in the barn stuffed by hay, among other things, in the words of the following song: “Yes, I’ll milk myself, but I won’t see the tail” and “if you didn’t notice, they’re not udders, it’s tits.”
“Pump it out, pump it out, feed my babies, pump it out, pump it out, pump it out, I’ll milk my ladies.”
However, an advertisement published on the company's Facebook page sparked discussion online.
The video, which attracted 77 000 views and hundreds of comments, has sparked a contradictory reaction among viewers. Many say it's making fun of the dairy industry’s daily ‘horrors’.
“Using cows to promote this product was a very bad decision.
They need to get pregnant and give birth so that they can produce milk. However, their babies are stolen from them shortly after giving birth,” one commenter writes.
Elvie breast pump fits discreetly inside the breastfeeding vest (Elvie/Mother)
Another viewer commented: “Understandably, it’s traumatic for both the mother and the baby.
But why can’t they be used to promote the breast pump to mothers who are allowed to keep their babies?”
The third commenter added: “A complete reality-driven advertisement.”
I also got defenders: a woman admitted that the advertisement was ‘hilarious’.
“I think that’s a great idea.
I'd get one if I'd still breastfeed.
Pumping made me feel like a cow.
It's a little crazy, but I took it as it is.
“It’s an ingenious product,” reads one of the comments.
Another viewer commented: “This is a funny advertisement aimed at mothers who pump (often in the workplace or in toilets) and ‘feel like cows’.
The purpose of the advertisement is not to praise or condemn the dairy industry.”
At the end of the advertisement, the women in the video reveal that they've danced discreet pumps on their bra stuffed.
The concept of an advertising campaign is based on the idea that many women using a breast pump feel like cows.
On the other hand, the Elvie pump is completely silent, has no wires and hoses and is discreetly suitable for breastfeeding vests, which gives women the freedom to move, hold the baby, and even go out during pumping.
Ana Balarin, partner and artistic director of the Mother advertising agency, commented: “The Elvie pump is a revolutionary product that deserved a bold and provocative entry into the market.
By juxtation, breastfeeding mothers and dairy cows, we wanted to bring all the challenges related to the use of the breast pump into the spotlight, we brought out the incredible sense of freedom that the new pump brought out in an entertaining and relatable way.”
The Elvie pump has made headlines before.
At the London Fashion Week, the mother of two appeared on the catwalk in designer Marta Jakubowski's outfit using the product.
Hundreds of immigrant children quietly moved to a tent camp on the Texas border
The number of migrant children arrested has increased sharply, even though the monthly number of border crossings has remained relatively unchanged. This is partly due to the fact that, as a result of the rough rhetoric and policies introduced by the Trump administration, the placement of children in foster families has become more difficult.
Most surrogative families have traditionally consisted of undocumented immigrants, and registering as a foster family may jeopardize their stay in the country.
The risk increased in June, when federal authorities announced that potential foster families and other adult members of the economy would have to provide their fingerprints, and that this information would be shared with immigration authorities.
Matthew Albence, a high-level immigration and customs authority, testified last week in Congress that the agency arrested dozens of people who had applied as surrogate families for underage immigrants.
The agency later confirmed that 70% of those detained had no previous criminal record.
“Almost 80% of people who are either members of a foster family or members of their households are in the country illegally, and a large of them are criminal foreigners.
So we will continue to look for these people,” Albence says.
In an effort to deal with the cases of children more quickly, the authorities have introduced new rules, according to which, in some cases, the case will have to be dealt with in the court within one month instead of the 60-day time limit that was previously in place of detention, the reception centre employees said.
Many people prefer to appear through a video conference rather than in person when they pursue their case in a court dealing with migrants.
Those who are not considered entitled to assistance will be expelled urgently.
According to reports published in recent months by reception center employees and the system, the time children spend in captivity contributes to whether children are starting to suffer from anxiety or depression, which can lead to violent outbursts or escape attempts.
According to lawyers, the concern is emphasized especially in larger establishments, such as Tornillo, where signs of a child's despair may go unnoticed due to the large size of the facility.
They add that the transfer of children to tent cities without being given enough time to prepare for it emotionally or say goodbye to friends can cause trauma, and that many of the children are suffering from trauma right now.
