The U.S. planned punishment for El Salvador for supporting China, but retrieved from its intentions
Diplomats have noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama have already quoted the situation in Beijing and Washington's slight counter-reaction.
President Trump had a warm-hearted meeting with Panamanian President Juan Carlos Varela in June 2017 and he also owned a hotel in Panama until his partners expelled the leadership team of the Trump Organization.
U.S. State Department officials decided to recall U.S. diplomatic leaders from El Salvador, the Dominican Republic, and Panama ‘on the basis of recent policies that Taiwan is no longer recognized’, said Heather Nauert, a spokesman for the department, in a statement earlier this month.
However, penalties were only considered for El Salvador, which received an estimated US$140 million in aid from the United States in 2017, including drug control, as well as development and financial support.
Proposed penalties, which included cuts in financial assistance and targeted visa restrictions, would have severely hit a Central American country with a high level of unemployment and homicidality.
As internal meetings progressed, the North and Central American authorities postponed a high-level meeting focusing on security and economic prosperity. The corresponding meeting of the previous year was seen as a steple in attempts to prevent migrants from running for the United States.
However, in mid-September, senior administrators made it clear that the meeting was to move forward and this effectively resolved speculation on the possible punishment of El Salvador.
“Vice President Mike Pence is now expected to give the mid-October meeting some indication of the importance the administration gives to the assembly,” diplomats said.
These three American ambassadors quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic without any nasty messages or punishments from Washington.
Bolton's White House spokesman declined to comment on the details of the conversation with three U.S. clerks, including two diplomats who agreed to discuss internal issues anonymously.
Their report was confirmed by an external analyst close to the administration, who also spoke under the protection of the anonymity condition.
History Teaches
The next inevitable step could be a report by special lawyer Robert Mueller on President Trump's possible obstruction of justice, which is now very weighty in public documents.
Mueller has also been reported to direct an investigation into whether Trump's campaign secretly cooperated with Russia when Russia interfered in U.S. elections.
If power changes in Congress, President Trump finds himself accountable to it just as he prepares to go in front of his constituents again, and is likely to be judged by his peers.
There are a lot of ifs here, and I don't mean to say that President Trump's defeat would be inevitable - any more than the defeat of similar people in Europe.
We need to make decisions on both sides of the Atlantic, and those solutions will determine how long this struggle will be.
In 1938, the German authorities would have been prepared to overthrew Hitler if the West had also opposed him and supported the Czechs in Munich.
We failed and missed the chance to avoid the years-long massacre that followed.
The course of history circulates at such turning points, where the inexorable forward march of democracy either intensifies or is delayed.
Americans are now facing a number of such turning points.
What will we do if President Trump dismisses Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, who is responsible for investigating the Mueller case?
Rosenstein has been grilled ever since it became known that he had proposed a secret recording of the president's speech and pondered the president's possible incompetence in the performance of his duties.
Rosenstein said The Times report was incorrect.
“How do we react if the recently requested FBI investigation into Brett Kavanugh’s activities isn’t complete or fair – or if he’s confirmed as a member of the Supreme Court, despite credible allegations of sexual harassment and dishonest testimony?”
And, most importantly, are we voting for a congress in the midterm elections that holds President Trump accountable?
If we fail in these attempts, our democracy will end up in a long and dark winter.
But I believe in our success because of the lesson I've learned in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovakian Jew and was deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that once filled my diplomatic home.
He survived, migrated to America and sent me 60 years later to light the sabbatical candles on that swastika decorated table.
How could this legacy have anything other than optimistic about the future?”
Norman Eisen is a Senior Research Fellow at the Brookings Institution and chairman of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington. He is the author of “The Last Palace: Europe’s Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House.”
Graham Dorrans of the Rangers with optimism about meeting Rapid Vienna
The Rangers will face Rapid Vienna at home on Thursday, knowing that earlier in the same month after a draw against Villarreal in Spain, a victory over the Austrians would give the team a strong position to qualify for Europa League Group G.
