Even though the study participants had a clear vision of how they hoped to take care of themselves, only a few had talked to their loved ones about it.
Approximately half of participants in the nationwide study said they were discussing the costs of long-term treatment with their spouse.
Only ten per cent of the respondents said that they had talked to their children about it.
“People want a family member to take care of them, but don’t bring it up,” said Holly Snyder, vice president of the national life insurance company.
Start like this.
Talk to your spouse and your children: you can't prepare your family to take care of you unless you share your wishes in time.
Discuss with a professional person and your family about where and how to get help, as these questions are an important factor in determining the cost.
Involve the financial advisor in the discussion: advice can also help you find a way to cover these costs.
Long-term care financing options may include, for example, traditional long-term care insurance, hybrid life insurance to cover these costs, or self-costed insurance if you can afford it.
Prepare legal documents: Prevent legal disputes.
Get a healthcare representative so you can designate a trusted person to supervise your medical care and to make sure your professionals follow your wishes in the event that you are unable to communicate with them.
Also, consider drawing up a power of attorney for the management of your financial affairs.
It allows you to assign a trusted person to take care of financial decisions on your behalf and make sure that your bills are paid if you are unable to take care of them.
Also, remember the small details: imagine that your elderly parent would be on the way to the hospital due to a health emergency.
Would you be able to answer questions about their medication and allergies?
Write such information into the plan so that you are prepared for the situation.
‘It’s not just a matter of finances. Who’s his doctor?’ Martin asks.
“Which drugs are in use?
Who takes care of the dog?
“Have the plan ready.”
