Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed in a powerful earthquake and tsunami that hit the town of Palu in Indonesia on Friday. Authorities say the number of victims is expected to increase.
Due to the closing of communications to the area, the rescue authorities have not received any information north of Palu from the municipality of Donggala, which is closer to the 7.5 magnitude quake's surface centre.
More than 16 000 people have been evacuated after the catastrophe.
Key information about Palus and Donggala on Sulawes Island:
Palu is the capital of the central province of Sulawes, located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of the island of Sulawes, with an estimated population of 379 800 in 2017.
An earthquake and a tsunami hit the city's 40th anniversary.
The municipality of Donggala extends more than 300 kilometers (180 miles) northwesterly to the island of Sulawes.
The municipality of Donggala is a provincial administrative area with an estimated population of 299 200 in 2017.
The main livelihoods of Central Sulawes Province, and in particular the Donggala coastal region, are fishing and farming.
Nicken mining is also important, but it is concentrated in Morowal on the opposite coast of Sulawes.
According to Indonesia's Disaster Mitigation Agency, Palu and Donggala have been hit by several tsunamis in the last hundred years.
In 1938, more than 200 people were killed by the tsunami and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
The tsunami also hit the western part of Donggala in 1996, killing nine people.
Indonesia is seismically active in the Pacific Ring of Fire and often suffers earthquakes.
Some of the most serious earthquakes and tsunamis of recent years:
2004: A powerful earthquake in northern Sumatra on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Acehi caused a tsunami on December 26 that struck 14 countries and killed 226,000 people on the coasts of the Indian Ocean. More than half of the victims were in the Aceh region.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes hit the west coast of Sumatra at the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people died on Nias Island in Sumatra's coastal waters.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude quake struck south of Indonesia's most populated island, Java, causing a tsunami that struck the south coast of the island, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6 magnitude quake struck the city of Padang, the capital of Western Sumatra.
More than 1 100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5 magnitude quake clashed one of Sumatra's Mentawai islands, causing a tsunami ten metres high, destroying several villages and killing about 300 people.
2016: The superficial quake hit Aceh's Kilike Jaya area, causing destruction and panic as people remember the huge damage caused by the 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
This time there was no tsunami, but more than a hundred people died when the buildings collapsed.
2018: Strong earthquakes hit Lombok tourist island in Indonesia and killed more than 500 people, mainly in the north of the island.
Thousands of buildings were destroyed by the Earthquake and left thousands of tourists temporarily isolated.
