Saipov, 30, used Home Depot's rental car in his October attack on the West Side Highway bike path in southern Manhattan, where he hit pedestrians and cyclists.
According to a report on the seizure of the death penalty in New York's southern jurisdiction, the death penalty will be possible if prosecutors are able to prove that Saipov “intentionally” killed eight of his victims and caused “intentionally” serious physical injuries.
According to the court document, it is possible to impose the death penalty for both of these charges.
Weeks after the attack, the federal tribunal fileded Saipovi with a 22-point indictment, which included charges of eight counts of murder to assist a terrorist organization. Federal prosecutors typically use this crime title in cases related to organized crime. In addition, the charges included charges of violence and the destruction of motor vehicles,
The attack required ‘significant planning and preparation’, prosecutors stated and described the way Saipov carried out his deeds as ‘horrific, cruel, and disgusting.’
“Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, inconvenience and loss to the families of Diego Enrique Angelin, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erliji, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco.
Five of the victims were Argentine tourists.
A decade has passed since New York's southern administrative tribunal has been sentenced to death.
Khalid Barnes was found guilty of the murders of two drug traffickers, but was eventually sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
The death penalty was last carried out in New York in a federal case in 1953, when married couple Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were sentenced for conspiracy for Soviet espionage during the Cold War two years earlier.
Both Rosenbergs were executed in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to court documents, Saipov, a citizen of Uzbekistan, showed no signs of remorse during the days and months following the attack.
“He told investigators his actions felt good,” the police said.
Saipov told the authorities that he was inspired by his attack after watching ISIS videos on his phone.
Police said Saipov also wanted an ISIS flag in his hospital room.
He responded to a 22-point indict that he was innocent.
David Patton, one of the federal public defense lawyers representing Saipov, said the defense was “naturally disappointed” with the prosecutor’s decision.
“In our opinion, the decision to demand the death penalty and no recognition and a life sentence without the possibility of release is a solution that will only prolong the tramage caused to those involved by these events,” Patton said.
Saipov's defence had previously asked him not to seek the death penalty.
Conservative MP says NIGEL FARAGE should be held accountable in the Brexit negotiations.
