How can the U.S.-China trade war benefit Beijing
“The starting point for the U.S.-China trade war was depraved, and even though the battle is far from over, a conflict between the two countries could benefit Beijing in the long term,” experts said.
U.S. President Donald Trump issued the first warning earlier this year by imposing import taxes on China's main export articles, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation occurred this week with the introduction of new tariffs, which will affect imported goods worth US$200 billion (150 billion pounds), effectively taxing half of all products imported from China to the United States.
Beijing has responded every time on the same scale, most recently by imposing tariffs of five ten percent on American imported goods worth US$60 billion.
China has pledged to respond to the United States eye-by-eye, and it doesn't seem that the world's second-largest economic power is about to fail for a moment.
In order for Washington to retreat, one would have to give in to our own demands, but public bowing to the United States would be far too humiliating for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
However, experts state that if Beijing plays its cards correctly, the U.S. trade war could have a positive impact on China in the long-term by reducing their interdependence.
“A speedy political decision, either in Washington or Beijing, can create the conditions that will embark on an economic downturn in either country, and this is, in fact, a more dangerous possibility than bystanders have understood so far,” said Abigail Gracé, an Asia-familiar research accompanist at the Center for New American Security.
According to the Syrian Foreign Minister, the country is “ready” for the return of refugees
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and asks for help to rebuild the country after more than seven years of devastating war.
Speaking to the General Assembly of the United Nations, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said the conditions in the country will improve.
“The situation is now more stable and safer thanks to developments in the fight against terrorism,” he said.
The government will continue to repair areas destroyed by terrorists in order to restore the normal situation.
Circumstances now allow refugees to return voluntarily to a country from which they were forced to leave because of terrorism and unilateral economic activities affecting their daily lives and livelihoods.
According to the U.N., more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country since the war began in 2011.
In addition, there are still six million people in need for humanitarian aid in the country.
Al-Moualem said that the Syrian government would be grateful for assistance in rebuilding the devastated country.
However, he insisted that the government would not accept conditional aid or assistance from states that supported the rebels.
The European team confirmed the Ryder Cup victory in Paris
The European team has won the 2018 Ryder Cup, defeating the U.S. team 16.5 against 10.5 on the Le Golf National course outside Paris, France.
The United States has now lost six times in a row in Europe. It hasn't won the Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
The European team kept the crown to itself when its Danish captain Thomas Bjørn's team scored 14.5 points, which was needed to defeat the United States.
U.S. star Phil Mickelson struggled almost throughout the tournament and hit his starting shot into threat on the par-line 16-hole, giving the match to Francesco Molinar.
Molinari of Italy excelled in all rounds and became one of the four players who has scored 5-0-0 since the tournaments started in their current form in 1979.
America's Jordan Spieth came out 5&4 at the bottom of the European team against Denmark's Thorbjørn Olesen.
Dustin Johnson, who was ranked as the world's top player, lost 2 and 1 to England's Ian Poulter, who could now play his last Ryder Cup.
Eight Ryder Cup golf veteran, Sergio Garcia of Spain, became the most victorious European of all time with 25.5 career points.
“I don’t usually cry, but I can’t help it today.
It's been a tough year.
I'm so grateful to Thomas that she chose me and believed in me.
I'm so happy to have this victory.
“It’s about the team and I’m glad I may have been able to help,” Said Garcia after the European team’s victory.
He will transfer the torch to compatriot Jon Rahm, who defeated U.S. golf legend Tiger Woods 2&1 in Sunday's individual competition.
“I’m feeling tremendous pride after defeating Tiger Woods. I grew up admiring him,” Rahm, 23-year-old, said.
Woods lost all four of his matches in France and now holds the Ryder Cup career record 13-21-3.
Weird stats from one of the greatest players of all time, who has won 14 big wins and remains second only to Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team struggled to find fairways throughout the weekend, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, whose games were of a high standard throughout the tournament.
U.S. team captain Jim Furyk said after a failed performance: ‘I’m proud of these guys, they fought.
We pressured the European team this morning's games.
We fought back.
Glory to Thomas.
He's an amazing captain.
All of his 12 players played very well.
We'll team up again, work with the American PGA and our Ryder Cup Committee, and we'll move on.
These 12 guys are awesome and I'll be proud to be their captain.
I'll have to raise my hat.
“Went to our winner.”
Situation of Red tide algae flowering: concentrations decrease in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota regions
According to the latest report from the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission, the number of algae is showing a general decline in the Tampa Bay region.
According to FWC, more fragmented algae areas are reported in the districts of Pinellas, Manatees, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, possibly indicating a decrease in concentrations.
Red Tide algae flowering extends along the coast for a distance of about 130 miles from Pinellas to the southern Lee counties.
Ferries can be spotted ten miles from Hillsborough county coast, but there are fewer of them than last week.
Red Tide algae flowering has also been observed in Pasco County.
Moderate concentrations were reported from Pinellas county's coast during the past week, concentrations ranged from shallow to talles on Hillsborough county coasts, background concentrations to high concentrations in Manates and Sarasota counties, from background concentration to moderate concentrations in Charlotte County, from background concentrations to high concentrations on the coasts of Lee's cubication, and Collier County concentrations were low.
Respiratory symptoms are further reported in the areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Over the past week, there were no reports of respiratory symptoms in the north-wester parts of Florida.
